mal Healthy State"], evidence:"Another study showed that overexpression of wild-type and LRRK2 (G2019S) in C. elegans was protective against rotenone toxicity while knockdown of endogenous LRK-1 by RNAi potentiated toxicity, suggesting a role for LRRK2 in mitochondrial regulation (Wolozin et al. 2008).", citationTitle:"J Neurosci Res. 2009 May 1;87(6):1283-95. doi: 10.1002/jnr.21949.", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"19025767", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19025767"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:562}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, co-expression of wild-type DJ-1 and PINK1 suppresses neurotoxin 1-methyl-4-phenylpyridinium (MPP+)-induced death of dopaminergic SH-SY5Y cells.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2006 Jun 1;15(11):1816-25. Epub 2006 Apr 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"16632486", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16632486"}}, {start: {_id:220}, end: {_id:562}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, co-expression of wild-type DJ-1 and PINK1 suppresses neurotoxin 1-methyl-4-phenylpyridinium (MPP+)-induced death of dopaminergic SH-SY5Y cells.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2006 Jun 1;15(11):1816-25. Epub 2006 Apr 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"16632486", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16632486"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:44}, properties:{evidence:"The mDA neurons from both twins had 50% GBA enzymatic activity, âˆšÂ¢Ã€ÃœÂ¬Âº3-fold elevated âˆšÃ©Â¬Â±-synuclein protein levels, and a reduced capacity to synthesize and release dopamine. Interestingly, the affected twin's neurons showed an even lower dopamine level, increased monoamine oxidase B (MAO-B) expression, and impaired intrinsic network activity.", citationTitle:"Cell Rep. 2014 Nov 20;9(4):1173-82. doi: 10.1016/j.celrep.2014.10.023. Epub 2014 Nov 6.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25456120", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25456120"}}, {start: {_id:1110}, end: {_id:77}, properties:{evidence:"Hyperactivation of N-methyl-d-aspartate receptors (NMDARs) leads to neuronal excitotoxicity and is suggested to play a role in many brain disorders, including Alzheimer's disease and schizophrenia.", citationComment:"Association analysis of GRIN1 and GRIN2B polymorphisms and Parkinson's disease in a hospital-based case-control study.", citationTitle:"Neuroscience letters; Vol. 478; Iss. 2", annotationSubgraph:["G-protein-mediated signaling"], annotationMeshdisease:["Brain Diseases"], citationPubDate:"2010-07-05", pmid:"20438806", citationAuthorList:"Shey-Lin Wu|Wen-Fu Wang|Hann-Yeh Shyu|Ying-Jui Ho|Jia-Ching Shieh|Yi-Ping Fu|Shwu-Tzy Wu|Chun-Wen Cheng", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20438806"}}] AS row
