MI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.024). (3) SNPs at two of the obesity risk loci (rs4836133 downstream of ZNF608; pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.002 and at rs713586 downstream of RBJ/DNAJC27; pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.018) were nominally associated with AD risk. Additionally, among the SNPs used for confirmation in both studies the AD risk allele of rs1858973, with an AD association just below genome-wide significance (pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢7.20â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-07)), was also associated with obesity (SNP at IQCK/GPRC5B; pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢5.21â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-06) ; pcorr â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢3.24â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-04)).", citationTitle:"Am J Med Genet B Neuropsychiatr Genet. 2014 Jun;165B(4):283-93. doi: 10.1002/ajmg.b.32234. Epub 2014 May 1.", annotationSubgraph:["G-protein-mediated signaling"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24788522", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24788522"}}, {start: {_id:1151}, end: {_id:1165}, properties:{evidence:"The AD SNP rs10838725 (pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢1.1â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-08)) at the locus CELF1 is also genome-wide significant for obesity (pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢7.35â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-09) ). (2) Four additional AD risk SNPs were nominally associated with obesity (rs17125944 at FERMT2, pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢4.03â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-05), pBMIâ€šÃ„Ã¢corr â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢2.50â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-03) ; rs3851179 at PICALM; pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.002, rs2075650 at TOMM40/APOE, pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.024, rs3865444 at CD33, pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.024). (3) SNPs at two of the obesity risk loci (rs4836133 downstream of ZNF608; pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.002 and at rs713586 downstream of RBJ/DNAJC27; pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢0.018) were nominally associated with AD risk. Additionally, among the SNPs used for confirmation in both studies the AD risk allele of rs1858973, with an AD association just below genome-wide significance (pAD â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢7.20â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-07)), was also associated with obesity (SNP at IQCK/GPRC5B; pBMI â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢5.21â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-06) ; pcorr â€šÃ„Ã¢=â€šÃ„Ã¢3.24â€šÃ„Ã¢âˆšÃ³â€šÃ„Ã¢10(-04)).", citationTitle:"Am J Med Genet B Neuropsychiatr Genet. 2014 Jun;165B(4):283-93. doi: 10.1002/ajmg.b.32234. Epub 2014 May 1.", annotationSubgraph:["G-protein-mediated signaling"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24788522", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24788522"}}, {start: {_id:16}, end: {_id:24}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Recently, in vitro studies of the PD-related proteins Parkin and PINK1 have found that these factors act in a common pathway to promote the selective autophagic degradation of damaged mitochondria (mitophagy).", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:16}, end: {_id:24}, properties:{evidence:"An inherited form of PD is caused by impaired function of Parkin, an E3 ubiquitin ligase reported to translocate to defective mitochondria in vitro to facilitate their clearance.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2011 Aug 2;108(31):12937-42. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1103295108. Epub 2011 Jul 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"21768369", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21768369"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:779}, end: {_id:515}, properties:{evidence:"Parkinson's disease is a movement disorder characterized by degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta. Dopaminergic neuronal loss also occurs in Drosophila melanogaster upon directed expression of alpha-synuclein, a protein implicated in the pathogenesis of Parkinson's disease and a major component of proteinaceous Lewy bodies. We report that directed expression of the molecular chaperone Hsp70 prevented dopaminergic neuronal loss associated with alpha-synuclein in Drosophila and that interference with endogenous chaperone activity accelerated alpha- synuclein toxicity. Furthermore, Lewy bodies in human postmortem tissue immunostained for molecular chaperones, also suggesting that chaperones may play a role in Parkinson's disease progression.", citationTitle:"Science. 2002 Feb 1;295(5556):865-8. Epub 2001 Dec 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"11823645", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11823645"}}, {start: {_id:1137}, end: {_id:55}, properties:{evidence:"In the present study, we examined whether interleukin-10 (IL-10, 1082G/A), interleukin-17A (IL-17A) rs8193036, rs2275913 and interferon-Å’â‰¥ (IFN-Å’â‰¥) polymorphisms were associated with the risk of cognitive impairment in PD.", citationComment:"Polymorphisms in immune/inflammatory cytokine genes are related to Parkinson's disease with cognitive impairment in the Han Chinese population.", citationTitle:"Neuroscience letters; Vol. 541; Iss.", annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], citationPubDate:"2013-04-29", pmid:"23485736", citationAuthorList:"Kun Nie|Yuhu Zhang|Rong Gan|Limin Wang|Jiehao Zhao|Zhiheng Huang|Hongmei Tang|Lijuan Wang", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23485736"}}, {start: {_id:1138}, end: {_id:55}, properties:{evidence:"In the present study, we examined whether interleukin-10 (IL-10, 1082G/A), interleukin-17A (IL-17A) rs8193036, rs2275913 and interferon-Å’â‰¥ (IFN-Å’â‰¥) polymorphisms were associated with the risk of cognitive impairment in PD.", citationComment:"Polymorphisms in immune/inflammatory cytokine genes are related to Parkinson's disease with cognitive impairment in the Han Chinese population.", citationTitle:"Neuroscience letters; Vol. 541; Iss.", annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], citationPubDate:"2013-04-29", annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"23485736", citationAuthorList:"Kun Nie|Yuhu Zhang|Rong Gan|Limin Wang|Jiehao Zhao|Zhiheng Huang|Hongmei Tang|Lijuan Wang", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23485736"}}, {start: {_id:1184}, end: {_id:55}, properties:{evidence:"Fragile X syndrome is a neurodevelopmental disorder that is caused by large methylated expansions of a CGG repeat (>200) region upstream of the FMR1 gene that results in the lack of expression of the fragile X mental retardation protein (FMRP). Affected individuals display a neurobehavioral phenotype that includes a significant impairment in social cognition alongside deficits in attentional control, inhibition and working memory. In contrast, relatively little is known about the trajectory and specificity of any cognitive impairment associated with the fragile X premutation (carrier-status) (approximately 55-200 repeats). Here, we focus on one aspect of cognition that has been well documented in the fragile X full mutation, namely social cognition. The results suggest that premutation males display a pattern of deficit similar in profile, albeit milder in presentation, to that of the full mutation. However, little evidence emerged for a correlation between CGG repeat length and severity of phenotypic outcomes. The findings are discussed in the context of functional neuroimaging and brain-behaviour-molecular correlates. We speculate that the deficiencies in social cognition are attributable to impairment of neural pathways modulated by the cerebellum.", citationTitle:"Brain Cogn. 2005 Feb;57(1):53-60.", annotationSubgraph:["FMR1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Brain Diseases"], pmid:"15629215", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15629215"}}, {start: {_id:1188}, end: {_id:55}, properties:{evidence:"We found that the enrichment of a rare chromosome 22q11.22 deletion in a recently expanded Northern Finnish sub-isolate enabled the detection of association between TOP3B and both schizophrenia and cognitive impairment. Biochemical analysis of TOP3Å’â‰¤ revealed that this topoisomerase was a component of cytosolic messenger ribonucleoproteins (mRNPs) and was catalytically active on RNA. The recruitment of TOP3Å’â‰¤ to mRNPs was independent of RNA cis-elements and was coupled to the co-recruitment of FMRP, the disease gene product in fragile X mental retardation syndrome. Our results indicate a previously unknown role for TOP3Å’â‰¤ in mRNA metabolism and suggest that it is involved in neurodevelopmental disorders.", citationTitle:"Nat Neurosci. 2013 Sep;16(9):1228-37.", annotationSubgraph:["Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"23912948", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23912948"}}, {start: {_id:24}, end: {_id:400}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Recently, in vitro studies of the PD-related proteins Parkin and PINK1 have found that these factors act in a common pathway to promote the selective autophagic degradation of damaged mitochondria (mitophagy).", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:1770}, end: {_id:1427}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"However, LC3-positive autophagic vesicles and immunoreactivity to the autophagic adaptor SQSTM1/p62 were rarely associated with mitochondrial clusters in cells coproducing HSD17B10 and Parkin, indicating an absence of mitophagy in these cells", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2015 Oct;22(10):1563-76. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2014.224. Epub 2015 Jan 16.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitophagy subgraph"], pmid:"25591737", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25591737"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:DECREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:43}, end: {_id:47}, properties:{evidence:"DDC is located next to the imprinted gene GRB10, which is expressed specifically from the paternal allele in fetal brains. Increased transmission of paternal DDC alleles has also been suggested in attention deficit hyperactivity disorder. We suggest that DDC might confer susceptibility to BPAD predominantly when paternally transmitted.", citationTitle:"Am J Med Genet B Neuropsychiatr Genet. 2003 Feb;117B(1):18-22.", annotationSubgraph:["GRB10 subgraph", "Dopaminergic subgraph"], pmid:"12555230", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12555230"}}, {start: {_id:300}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that PGAM5 negatively regulates the PINK1 pathway related to maintenance of the mitochondria and, furthermore, that PGAM5 acts between PINK1 and Parkin, or functions independently of Parkin downstream of PINK1.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that PGAM5 negatively regulates the PINK1 pathway related to maintenance of the mitochondria and, furthermore, that PGAM5 acts between PINK1 and Parkin, or functions independently of Parkin downstream of PINK1.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:300}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{evidence:"Here we show that a mitochondrial protein, phosphoglycerate mutase 5 (PGAM5, Gene ID: 192111), which was previously reported to be localized at the outer mitochondrial membrane and to lack a phosphoglycerate mutase activity [17], [18], is involved in the PINK1 pathway, and that loss of PGAM5 activity improves mitochondrial defects caused by PINK1 inactivation in Drosophila.Insulin substrate-4 (IRS-4, GeneID: 8471) and PGAM5 (Figure 1A).", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:304}, end: {_id:300}, properties:{evidence:"Here we show that a mitochondrial protein, phosphoglycerate mutase 5 (PGAM5, Gene ID: 192111), which was previously reported to be localized at the outer mitochondrial membrane and to lack a phosphoglycerate mutase activity [17], [18], is involved in the PINK1 pathway, and that loss of PGAM5 activity improves mitochondrial defects caused by PINK1 inactivation in Drosophila.Insulin substrate-4 (IRS-4, GeneID: 8471) and PGAM5 (Figure 1A).", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:307}, end: {_id:300}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Although we confirmed the association of PGAM5 with Keap1 in human cultured cells, the proposed Keap1-binding motif in PGAM5, NXESGE, was not conserved in dPGAM5 .", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:300}, end: {_id:309}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Therefore, it seems possible that PGAM5 modulates a cell protective or a mitochondrial morphogenetic activity of the Bcl-2 family member Bcl- xL downstream of PINK1 but in a pathway that is independent from Parkin .", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Bcl-2 subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:220}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Although the causative genes for autosomal recessive PD, Parkin, PINK1 and DJ-1, share a common pathway, at least in part, in mitochondrial quality control and protein quality control, their precise relationship remains elusive.", citationTitle:"Neurosci Res. 2013 Dec;77(4):228-33. doi: 10.1016/j.neures.2013.09.006. Epub 2013 Sep 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"24064392", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24064392"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Although the causative genes for autosomal recessive PD, Parkin, PINK1 and DJ-1, share a common pathway, at least in part, in mitochondrial quality control and protein quality control, their precise relationship remains elusive.", citationTitle:"Neurosci Res. 2013 Dec;77(4):228-33. doi: 10.1016/j.neures.2013.09.006. Epub 2013 Sep 21.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"24064392", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24064392"}}, {start: {_id:321}, end: {_id:203}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, it has been shown that LRRK2 regulates mitochondrial dynamics by increasing mitochondrial DLP1 ( dynamin-like kinase 1) through a direct interaction with DLP1.", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:321}, end: {_id:324}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, it has been shown that LRRK2 regulates mitochondrial dynamics by increasing mitochondrial DLP1 ( dynamin-like kinase 1) through a direct interaction with DLP1.", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:368}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The parkin gene is mildly homologous to ubiquitin at the N-terminal portion and has a RING-finger motif at the C-terminal portion.", citationTitle:"Rinsho Shinkeigaku. 1999 Dec;39(12):1259-61.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"10791092", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10791092"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:372}, properties:{evidence:"Alpha-synuclein is involved in a rare dominant form of familial PD with dopa responsive parkinsonian features and Lewy body positive pathology.", citationTitle:"IJ Neural Transm Suppl. 2000;(60):101-16.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"11205133", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11205133"}}, {start: {_id:375}, end: {_id:325}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The expression domain of uch-L1 overlaps with that of tyrosine hydroxylase, a molecular marker for dopaminergic neurons, in the ventral diencephalon, an equivalent structure to the substantia nigra where PD progresses in human.", citationTitle:"Biochem Biophys Res Commun. 2003 Dec 19;312(3):601-7.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Dopaminergic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Prosencephalon", "Substantia Nigra", "Dopaminergic Neurons"], pmid:"14680807", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"14680807"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:381}, properties:{evidence:"Whole gene duplications and triplications of alpha-synuclein (SNCA) can cause Parkinson's disease (PD), and variation in the promoter region (Rep1) and 3' region of SNCA has been reported to increase disease susceptibility.", citationTitle:"Mov Disord. 2009 Jun 15;24(8):1125-31. doi: 10.1002/mds.22524.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"19412953", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19412953"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:395}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We found that parkin protein was a ubiquitin-protein ligase of the ubiquitin system.", citationTitle:"Rinsho Shinkeigaku. 2004 Apr-May;44(4-5):241-62.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"15287506", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15287506"}}, {start: {_id:388}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We found evidence to indicate accumulation of PAEL receptor and the presence of endoplasmic reticulum stress in a patient with AR-JP (Park2).", citationTitle:"Rinsho Shinkeigaku. 2004 Apr-May;44(4-5):241-62.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"15287506", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15287506"}}, {start: {_id:415}, end: {_id:414}, properties:{evidence:"GDNF is the neurotrophic factor that has attracted the most attention in terms of its potential as a neuroprotective/regenerative therapy for PD.", citationTitle:"Neural Regen Res. 2014 Oct 1;9(19):1708-11. doi: 10.4103/1673-5374.143410.", annotationSubgraph:["Neurotrophic subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25422631", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25422631"}}, {start: {_id:427}, end: {_id:438}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Multiple kinases have been identified which phosphorylate alpha-syn at S129, with most evidence pointing to polo-like kinase 2 (PLK2) as the primary phosphorylated of alpha-syn S129.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:439}, end: {_id:441}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Few studies have sought to identify phosphatases of alpha-syn ; however, concurring data point to PP2A as a major phosphatase of the S129 site.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:457}, end: {_id:209}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"This is further supported by the work of Dzamko et al. (2012) who show that inhibitor of kappa B kinases (IKKs) phosphorylate LRRK2 in bone marrow-derived macrophages upon activation of toll-like receptor signaling which is specific to immune cells.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Cytokine signaling subgraph", "LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:452}, end: {_id:203}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The cellular sites (S935) and cellular autophosphorylation sites (S1292) are the most physiologically relevant and the comparison of both sites suggests that these may be useful indicators of LRRK2 activity or pathology.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:459}, end: {_id:203}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The cellular sites (S935) and cellular autophosphorylation sites (S1292) are the most physiologically relevant and the comparison of both sites suggests that these may be useful indicators of LRRK2 activity or pathology.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:460}, end: {_id:209}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We recently reported that protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) is a main phosphatase of the LRRK2 ANK-LRR interdomain sites.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph", "Lipid metabolism subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:419}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Interestingly, the injection of alpha-syn fibrillar strains into mouse brain is shown to induce tau aggregation (Guo et al., 2013), suggesting an interconnection between alpha-syn and tau pathological mechanisms.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:203}, end: {_id:441}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Qing and colleagues reported that crude lysates of E. coli expressing LRRK2 as source of enzyme could phosphorylate alpha-syn at S129, suggesting that LRRK2 may regulate alpha-syn phosphorylation in conjunction with bacterial proteins (Qing et al., 2009), although this is unlikely to be representative of a human physiological situation.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:796}, end: {_id:798}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Of note, a possible LRRK2 missense mutation, Q930R, reported in a European PD case, localizes within the phosphorylation cluster nearby the S935 phosphorylated residue (29).", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2007 Jan 15;16(2):223-32. Epub 2007 Jan 2.", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"17200152", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17200152"}}, {start: {_id:906}, end: {_id:203}, properties:{evidence:"The cellular sites (S935) and cellular autophosphorylation sites (S1292) are the most physiologically relevant and the comparison of both sites suggests that these may be useful indicators of LRRK2 activity or pathology.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:461}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["GSK3 subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:462}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Cell cycle subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:907}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Energy metabolic subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:464}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Wnt signaling subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:465}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:466}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:908}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Endoplasmic reticulum-Golgi protein export", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:467}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Cell cycle subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:468}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:470}, end: {_id:424}, properties:{evidence:"Several kinases have been reported to phosphorylate tau. These include proline directed kinases [glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK3), cyclin dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), and 5- adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK)], non-proline directed kinases [casein kinase 1 (CK1), microtubule affinity regulating kinases (MARKs), death associated protein kinase 1 (DAPK1), cyclic AMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA) and dual specificity tyrosine-phosphorylation regulated kinase 1A (DYRK-1A)] as well tyrosine kinases including Fyn, Abl, and Syk.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:203}, end: {_id:427}, properties:{evidence:"Qing and colleagues reported that crude lysates of E. coli expressing LRRK2 as source of enzyme could phosphorylate alpha-syn at S129, suggesting that LRRK2 may regulate alpha-syn phosphorylation in conjunction with bacterial proteins (Qing et al., 2009), although this is unlikely to be representative of a human physiological situation.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{evidence:"Here we show that mutations in PINK1 (PTEN-induced kinase 1) are associated with PARK6. We have identified two homozygous mutations affecting the PINK1 kinase domain in three consanguineous PARK6 families: a truncating nonsense mutation and a missense mutation at a highly conserved amino acid.", citationTitle:"Science. 2004 May 21;304(5674):1158-60. Epub 2004 Apr 15.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"15087508", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15087508"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:659}, end: {_id:137}, properties:{evidence:"Cyclin G-associated kinase (GAK) modifies alpha-synuclein expression levels and affects the susceptibility of Parkinson's disease (PD). The single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) rs1564282 of GAK gene has a significant association to the risk of PD among Caucasian populations.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2013 Jun 24;8(6):e67506. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0067506. Print 2013.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"23826309", citationType:"PubMed", annotationRace:["Caucasian"], citationRef:"23826309"}}, {start: {_id:1481}, end: {_id:1445}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"we found that PINK1 directly interacts with Tom20, the main receptor for N-terminal presequences", citationTitle:"Dev Cell. 2012 Feb 14;22(2):320-33. doi: 10.1016/j.devcel.2011.12.014. Epub 2012 Jan 25.", annotationSpecies:["7227"], pmid:"22280891", annotationCellline:["SH-SY5Y"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22280891"}}, {start: {_id:1489}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"TOMM7 as essential for stabilizing PINK1 on the outer mitochondrial membrane following mitochondrial damage", citationTitle:"Dev Cell. 2012 Feb 14;22(2):320-33. doi: 10.1016/j.devcel.2011.12.014. Epub 2012 Jan 25.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], pmid:"22280891", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22280891"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1519}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The investigation of the role of key elements of the autophagy machinery in our study confirmed that OMM proteolysis triggered by PINK1 and PARK2 relies on the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway; in addition, it demonstrated that PARK2 cooperates subsequently with ATG5, ATG7, and AMBRA1 in the absence of PINK1 to promote the degradation of mitochondrial remnants.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1801-17. doi: 10.4161/auto.25884. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24149440", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149440"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1520}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The investigation of the role of key elements of the autophagy machinery in our study confirmed that OMM proteolysis triggered by PINK1 and PARK2 relies on the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway; in addition, it demonstrated that PARK2 cooperates subsequently with ATG5, ATG7, and AMBRA1 in the absence of PINK1 to promote the degradation of mitochondrial remnants.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1801-17. doi: 10.4161/auto.25884. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24149440", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149440"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1521}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The investigation of the role of key elements of the autophagy machinery in our study confirmed that OMM proteolysis triggered by PINK1 and PARK2 relies on the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway; in addition, it demonstrated that PARK2 cooperates subsequently with ATG5, ATG7, and AMBRA1 in the absence of PINK1 to promote the degradation of mitochondrial remnants.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1801-17. doi: 10.4161/auto.25884. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24149440", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149440"}}, {start: {_id:421}, end: {_id:1523}, properties:{evidence:"Vps35, along with Vps26 and Vps29, forms the so-called cargo recognition complex of the retromer", citationTitle:"EMBO J. 2014 Feb 18;33(4):282-95. doi: 10.1002/embj.201385902. Epub 2014 Jan 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Endoplasmic reticulum-Golgi protein export"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24446486", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24446486"}}, {start: {_id:421}, end: {_id:1524}, properties:{evidence:"Vps35, along with Vps26 and Vps29, forms the so-called cargo recognition complex of the retromer", citationTitle:"EMBO J. 2014 Feb 18;33(4):282-95. doi: 10.1002/embj.201385902. Epub 2014 Jan 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Endoplasmic reticulum-Golgi protein export"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24446486", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24446486"}}, {start: {_id:1525}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"we identified HK2 as a modifier of parkin recruitment to mitochondria prior to mitophagy in both transformed cells and neurons. Mechanistically, we show that the hexokinase activity of HK2 is critical and PINK1 must be expressed. Furthermore, we establish that Akt contributes to recruitment of parkin via phosphorylation of T473, resulting in recruitment of HK2 to the OMM.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2014 Jan 1;23(1):145-56. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddt407. Epub 2013 Aug 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Energy metabolic subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"23962723", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23962723"}}, {start: {_id:1441}, end: {_id:1446}, properties:{evidence:"In this study, we show that MUL1 suppresses PINK1 or parkin phenotypes in Drosophila. The suppression is achieved through the ubiquitin- dependent degradation of Mitofusin, which itself causes PINK1/parkin mutant-like toxicity when overexpressed. We further show that removing MUL1 in PINK1 or parkin loss-of-function mutant aggravates phenotypes caused by loss of either gene alone, leading to lethality in flies and degeneration in mouse cortical neurons. Together, these observations show that MUL1 acts in parallel to the PINK1/parkin pathway on a shared target mitofusin to maintain mitochondrial integrity. The MUL1 pathway compensates for loss of PINK1/parkin in both Drosophila and mammals and is a promising therapeutic target for PD", citationTitle:"Elife. 2014 Jun 4;3:e01958. doi: 10.7554/eLife.01958.", annotationSpecies:["7227"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24898855", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24898855"}}, {start: {_id:1441}, end: {_id:1445}, properties:{evidence:"In this study, we show that MUL1 suppresses PINK1 or parkin phenotypes in Drosophila. The suppression is achieved through the ubiquitin- dependent degradation of Mitofusin, which itself causes PINK1/parkin mutant-like toxicity when overexpressed. We further show that removing MUL1 in PINK1 or parkin loss-of-function mutant aggravates phenotypes caused by loss of either gene alone, leading to lethality in flies and degeneration in mouse cortical neurons. Together, these observations show that MUL1 acts in parallel to the PINK1/parkin pathway on a shared target mitofusin to maintain mitochondrial integrity. The MUL1 pathway compensates for loss of PINK1/parkin in both Drosophila and mammals and is a promising therapeutic target for PD", citationTitle:"Elife. 2014 Jun 4;3:e01958. doi: 10.7554/eLife.01958.", annotationSpecies:["7227"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24898855", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24898855"}}, {start: {_id:1514}, end: {_id:1553}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The TOM complex contains three receptor proteins: Tom20, Tom22, and Tom70.The TOM precursors, including the precursor of Tom22, have to be imported into the mitochondrial outer membrane. CK2 phosphorylates the Tom22 precursor in the cytosol and stimulates its import into mitochondria. Tom20 has been shown to function as receptor for the precursor of Tom22. Phosphorylation of Tom22 by CK2 stimulates its binding to Tom20 and thus promotes its import", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2014 Mar 4;19(3):357-72. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.01.010. Epub 2014 Feb 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24561263", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24561263"}}, {start: {_id:1560}, end: {_id:1561}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"NRF-2 belongs to the ETS family; these factors regulate a large number of mitochondrial genes encoded in the cell nucleus, including mtTFA", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1591}, end: {_id:1587}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Included in the ATFS-1 program were many genes with roles in protecting against mitochondrial dysfunction (Fig. 4C) including the mitochondrial chaperones dnj-10 (14) (Fig. 4D) and hsp-60 (Fig. S13B). Numerous components involved in reactive oxygen species detoxification required ATFS-1 for induction during stress including the transcription factor skn-1 (15) (Fig. 4E). Several glycolysis genes including gpd-2 (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase) were also induced (Fig. 4F) suggesting the UPRmt may contribute to a shift in ATP production from respiration to glycolysis. ATFS-1 was also required for the induction of tim-23 and tim-17 (Figs. 4G & S13C); core components of the TIM23 complex (1).", citationTitle:"Science. 2012 Aug 3;337(6094):587-90. doi: 10.1126/science.1223560. Epub 2012 Jun 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Transcription subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"22700657", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22700657"}}, {start: {_id:1592}, end: {_id:1587}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Included in the ATFS-1 program were many genes with roles in protecting against mitochondrial dysfunction (Fig. 4C) including the mitochondrial chaperones dnj-10 (14) (Fig. 4D) and hsp-60 (Fig. S13B). Numerous components involved in reactive oxygen species detoxification required ATFS-1 for induction during stress including the transcription factor skn-1 (15) (Fig. 4E). Several glycolysis genes including gpd-2 (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase) were also induced (Fig. 4F) suggesting the UPRmt may contribute to a shift in ATP production from respiration to glycolysis. ATFS-1 was also required for the induction of tim-23 and tim-17 (Figs. 4G & S13C); core components of the TIM23 complex (1).", citationTitle:"Science. 2012 Aug 3;337(6094):587-90. doi: 10.1126/science.1223560. Epub 2012 Jun 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Disaccharide metabolism subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"22700657", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22700657"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1611}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Mutations in PTEN-induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) or parkin cause autosomal recessive forms of Parkinson's disease.Recent work suggests that loss of mitochondrial membrane potential stabilizes PINK1 and that accumulated PINK1 recruits parkin from the cytoplasm to mitochondria for elimination of depolarized mitochondria, which is known as mitophagy.In this study, we find that PINK1 forms a complex with sterile ÃŽÂ± and TIR motif containing 1 (SARM1) and tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor 6 (TRAF6), which is important for import of PINK1 in the outer membrane and stabilization of PINK1 on depolarized mitochondria. SARM1, which is known to be an adaptor protein for Toll-like receptor, binds to PINK1 and promotes TRAF6-mediated lysine 63 chain ubiquitination of PINK1 at lysine 433. Down-regulation of SARM1 and TRAF6 abrogates accumulation of PINK1, followed by recruitment of parkin to damaged mitochondria. Some pathogenic mutations of PINK1 reduce the complex formation and ubiquitination.These results indicate that association of PINK1 with SARM1 and TRAF6 is an important step for mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Mol Biol Cell. 2013 Sep;24(18):2772-84. doi: 10.1091/mbc.E13-01-0016. Epub 2013 Jul 24.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"23885119", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23885119"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1612}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Mutations in PTEN-induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) or parkin cause autosomal recessive forms of Parkinson's disease.Recent work suggests that loss of mitochondrial membrane potential stabilizes PINK1 and that accumulated PINK1 recruits parkin from the cytoplasm to mitochondria for elimination of depolarized mitochondria, which is known as mitophagy.In this study, we find that PINK1 forms a complex with sterile ÃŽÂ± and TIR motif containing 1 (SARM1) and tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor 6 (TRAF6), which is important for import of PINK1 in the outer membrane and stabilization of PINK1 on depolarized mitochondria. SARM1, which is known to be an adaptor protein for Toll-like receptor, binds to PINK1 and promotes TRAF6-mediated lysine 63 chain ubiquitination of PINK1 at lysine 433. Down-regulation of SARM1 and TRAF6 abrogates accumulation of PINK1, followed by recruitment of parkin to damaged mitochondria. Some pathogenic mutations of PINK1 reduce the complex formation and ubiquitination.These results indicate that association of PINK1 with SARM1 and TRAF6 is an important step for mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Mol Biol Cell. 2013 Sep;24(18):2772-84. doi: 10.1091/mbc.E13-01-0016. Epub 2013 Jul 24.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"23885119", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23885119"}}, {start: {_id:1593}, end: {_id:1555}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Tim17A is a stress-sensitive subunit of the TIM23 protein import complex. Tim17A protein decreases in response to stress-regulated translation attenuation. Tim17A degradation requires the activity of the mitochondrial protease YME1L. Decreased Tim17A/Tim-17 is protective against oxidative stress", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2013 Dec 3;18(6):908-19. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2013.11.006.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph"], pmid:"24315374", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24315374"}}, {start: {_id:1655}, end: {_id:394}, properties:{evidence:"While ROS are required for glucose metabolism and metastasis in triple-negative breast cancers (9), decreased SirT3 levels concomitant with high ROS levels are frequently observed in all breast cancers (8). Based on these findings, SirT3 is considered a tumor suppressor (5, 8). SirT3 regulates the activity of magnesium superoxide dismutase (MnSOD), which detoxifies superoxide to hydrogen peroxide (5, 7, 10,â€“14). Moreover, SirT3 has been linked to augmented transcription of MnSOD and catalase, both known targets of the transcription factor FOXO3A (15). The SirT3-dependent deacetylation of FOXO3A promotes both its nuclear translocation and its transcriptional activity (16, 17). Therefore, mechanistically, the reduction of SirT3 levels leads to an elevation in ROS levels by compromising the mitochondrial antioxidant machinery.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Response to oxidative stress"], annotationMeshdisease:["Breast Neoplasms"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1655}, end: {_id:1658}, properties:{evidence:"While ROS are required for glucose metabolism and metastasis in triple-negative breast cancers (9), decreased SirT3 levels concomitant with high ROS levels are frequently observed in all breast cancers (8). Based on these findings, SirT3 is considered a tumor suppressor (5, 8). SirT3 regulates the activity of magnesium superoxide dismutase (MnSOD), which detoxifies superoxide to hydrogen peroxide (5, 7, 10,â€“14). Moreover, SirT3 has been linked to augmented transcription of MnSOD and catalase, both known targets of the transcription factor FOXO3A (15). The SirT3-dependent deacetylation of FOXO3A promotes both its nuclear translocation and its transcriptional activity (16, 17). Therefore, mechanistically, the reduction of SirT3 levels leads to an elevation in ROS levels by compromising the mitochondrial antioxidant machinery.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Apoptosis signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Breast Neoplasms"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1660}, end: {_id:1663}, properties:{evidence:"USP15 has previously been shown to activate the TGF-Î² pathway through deubiquitination of the type I TGF-Î² receptor and receptor-activated SMAD proteins (26,27).", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2014 Oct 1;23(19):5227-42. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddu244. Epub 2014 May 22.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24852371", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24852371"}}, {start: {_id:1665}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{evidence:"We recently reported that ataxinâ€�3, a DUB responsible for Machadoâ€�Joseph's disease, can deubiquitinate parkin (Durcan et al, 2011, 2012). However, the effects of ataxinâ€�3 on parkinâ€�mediated mitophagy were not investigated.", citationTitle:"EMBO J. 2014 Nov 3;33(21):2473-91. doi: 10.15252/embj.201489729. Epub 2014 Sep 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"25216678", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25216678"}}, {start: {_id:1668}, end: {_id:1666}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These findings suggest that SKN-1 may control mitophagy through regulation of dct-1 transcription. Indeed, skn-1 knockdown impairs mitochondrial network morphology, causes mitochondrial membrane depolarization and increases cytoplasmic Ca2+ concentration (Fig. 4dâ€“f and Extended Data Fig. 10g), similarly to DCT-1 deficiency.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 May 28;521(7553):525-8. doi: 10.1038/nature14300. Epub 2015 Apr 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitophagy subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"25896323", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25896323"}}, {start: {_id:1666}, end: {_id:1673}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In our recent study, we demonstrate that DCT-1, the Caenorhabditis elegans homolog of the mammalian BNIP3 and BNIP3L/NIX, is a key mediator of mitophagy, and functions in the same genetic pathway with PINK-1 and PDR-1 (the nematode homologs of PINK1 and Parkin respectively) to promote longevity and prevent cell damage under stress conditions. Interestingly, accumulation of damaged mitochondria activates SKN-1 (SKiNhead-1), the nematode homolog of NRF2, which in turn initiates a compensatory retrograde signaling response that impinges on both mitochondrial biogenesis and removal.", citationTitle:"Worm. 2015 Jul 29;4(3):e1071763. doi: 10.1080/21624054.2015.1071763.", annotationSubgraph:["Bcl-2 subgraph"], pmid:"26430570", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26430570"}}, {start: {_id:1666}, end: {_id:1674}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In our recent study, we demonstrate that DCT-1, the Caenorhabditis elegans homolog of the mammalian BNIP3 and BNIP3L/NIX, is a key mediator of mitophagy, and functions in the same genetic pathway with PINK-1 and PDR-1 (the nematode homologs of PINK1 and Parkin respectively) to promote longevity and prevent cell damage under stress conditions. Interestingly, accumulation of damaged mitochondria activates SKN-1 (SKiNhead-1), the nematode homolog of NRF2, which in turn initiates a compensatory retrograde signaling response that impinges on both mitochondrial biogenesis and removal.", citationTitle:"Worm. 2015 Jul 29;4(3):e1071763. doi: 10.1080/21624054.2015.1071763.", pmid:"26430570", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26430570"}}, {start: {_id:1666}, end: {_id:1670}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In our recent study, we demonstrate that DCT-1, the Caenorhabditis elegans homolog of the mammalian BNIP3 and BNIP3L/NIX, is a key mediator of mitophagy, and functions in the same genetic pathway with PINK-1 and PDR-1 (the nematode homologs of PINK1 and Parkin respectively) to promote longevity and prevent cell damage under stress conditions. Interestingly, accumulation of damaged mitochondria activates SKN-1 (SKiNhead-1), the nematode homolog of NRF2, which in turn initiates a compensatory retrograde signaling response that impinges on both mitochondrial biogenesis and removal.", citationTitle:"Worm. 2015 Jul 29;4(3):e1071763. doi: 10.1080/21624054.2015.1071763.", pmid:"26430570", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26430570"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:216}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 is involved in the phosphorylation of the mitochondrial proteins TRAP1 (TNF receptor-associated protein 1), Htr2a (5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) receptor 2a)/OMI (Plun-Favreau et al, 2007; Pridgeon et al, 2007) and also Parkin (Kim et al, 2008), and associates with Hsp90 (heat shock protein 90)/Cdc37 (cell division cycle 37) chaperones (Moriwaki et al, 2008; Weihofen et al, 2007).", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1714}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 is involved in the phosphorylation of the mitochondrial proteins TRAP1 (TNF receptor-associated protein 1), Htr2a (5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) receptor 2a)/OMI (Plun-Favreau et al, 2007; Pridgeon et al, 2007) and also Parkin (Kim et al, 2008), and associates with Hsp90 (heat shock protein 90)/Cdc37 (cell division cycle 37) chaperones (Moriwaki et al, 2008; Weihofen et al, 2007).", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:634}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{evidence:"Human PINK1 (hPink1res) fully complemented Complex I enzymatic activity in Pink1âˆ’/âˆ’ mouse fibroblasts (Fig 5B and â€‹and6B).6B). In contrast, two PD PINK1 clinical mutations G309D (Gly309 â†’ Asp) and W437X (Trp437 â†’ Xxx) as well as an artificial mutation K219A (Lys219 â†’ Ala) that inactivates the kinase domain (Silvestri et al, 2005) were not effective", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In addition, we found evidence that both Parkin and PINK1 are involved in selective turnover of respiratory chain (RC) subunits.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In addition, we found evidence that both Parkin and PINK1 are involved in selective turnover of respiratory chain (RC) subunits.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Parkin Mediates Selective Turnover of Mitochondrial Respiratory Chain Proteins.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:1805}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Ubiquitin-dependent regulation of the TOM-dependent import of HSD17B10 by Parkin, possibly in cooperation with PINK1, may ensure the correct maintenance of HSD17B10 homeostasis in conditions of low mitochondrial import efficiency.", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2015 Oct;22(10):1563-76. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2014.224. Epub 2015 Jan 16.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"25591737", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25591737"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1845}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 Functionally Interacts with Pum and the TOM/TIM Complex to Regulate the Localization and Translation of nRCC mRNAs", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Post-translational events", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1846}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 Functionally Interacts with Pum and the TOM/TIM Complex to Regulate the Localization and Translation of nRCC mRNAs", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Post-translational events", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Parkin Cooperates with PINK1 to Promote hnRNP-F/Glo Ubiquitination and nRCC mRNA Translation", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1845}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Parkin associated with Pum-1 in HEK293 cells", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Post-translational events"], pmid:"25565208", annotationCellline:["HEK293"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:1839}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Third, Glo physically associated with Parkin in fly tissues", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Pre-translational events"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1850}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"A Ub-positive, higher-molecular-weight species consistent with monoubiquitinated Glo was detected in Glo-IP prepared from wild-type but not parkin or PINK1 mutant flies, suggesting that PINK1 and Parkin regulate Glo ubiquitination", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Pre-translational events"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1850}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"A Ub-positive, higher-molecular-weight species consistent with monoubiquitinated Glo was detected in Glo-IP prepared from wild-type but not parkin or PINK1 mutant flies, suggesting that PINK1 and Parkin regulate Glo ubiquitination", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Pre-translational events"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:807}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Interestingly, in PINK1 mutant expressing a phospho-mimetic, but not a nonphosphorylatable form of Parkin with the PINK1 phosphorylation site mutated (Shiba-Fukushima et al., 2014),Glo ubiquitination was restored (Figure S7G), indicating that PINK1 acts through Parkin to regulate Glo ubiqui- tination.", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:1850}, end: {_id:1857}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We hypothesize that ubiquitination of Glo alters the structure of the repressor complexes and their binding to nRCC mRNAs.", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Pre-translational events"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1553}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Our results implicate PINK1 in the localization of nRCC mRNAs to MOM. PINK1 may act in this process through its binding to nRCC mRNAs and through its interaction with Tom20.", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}, {start: {_id:1875}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"AIF interacts with CHCHD4, a regulator of the intermembrane space import machinery", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["4932"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:504}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"AIF interacts with CHCHD4, a regulator of the intermembrane space import machinery", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["4932"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1876}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"AIF interacts with CHCHD4, a regulator of the intermembrane space import machinery", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["4932"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:504}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These experiments revealed that both long isoforms of AIF, AIF1 and AIF2 (Hangen et al., 2010b), indistinguishably supported the expression of CHCHD4.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1875}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These experiments revealed that both long isoforms of AIF, AIF1 and AIF2 (Hangen et al., 2010b), indistinguishably supported the expression of CHCHD4.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1580}, end: {_id:1642}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Conceptually, c-Jun may terminate the ATF4-mediated upregulation of parkin expression by competing with ATF4 on the parkin promoter.", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2011 May;18(5):769-82. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2010.142. Epub 2010 Nov 26.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "MAPK subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"21113145", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21113145"}}, {start: {_id:1970}, end: {_id:216}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Consequently, this leads to the activation of TANK-binding kinase 1 (TBK1) and phosphorylation of interferon regulatory factor 3 (IRF3), both of which constitutively associate with cytosolic chaperone Hsp90. It remains largely unknown how MAVS activates TBK1/IRF3. In this study, we identified translocases of outer membrane 70 (Tom70), a mitochondrial import receptor, to interact with MAVS upon RNA virus infection. Ectopic expression or knockdown of Tom70 could enhance or impair IRF3-mediated gene expression, respectively.", citationTitle:"Cell Res. 2010 Sep;20(9):994-1011. doi: 10.1038/cr.2010.103. Epub 2010 Jul 13.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph"], pmid:"20628368", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20628368"}}, {start: {_id:1635}, end: {_id:216}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Consequently, this leads to the activation of TANK-binding kinase 1 (TBK1) and phosphorylation of interferon regulatory factor 3 (IRF3), both of which constitutively associate with cytosolic chaperone Hsp90. It remains largely unknown how MAVS activates TBK1/IRF3. In this study, we identified translocases of outer membrane 70 (Tom70), a mitochondrial import receptor, to interact with MAVS upon RNA virus infection. Ectopic expression or knockdown of Tom70 could enhance or impair IRF3-mediated gene expression, respectively.", citationTitle:"Cell Res. 2010 Sep;20(9):994-1011. doi: 10.1038/cr.2010.103. Epub 2010 Jul 13.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"20628368", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20628368"}}, {start: {_id:1981}, end: {_id:2003}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"NLRP3 is a key component of the macromolecular signaling complex called the inflammasome that promotes caspase 1-dependent production of IL-1beta. The adaptor ASC is necessary for NLRP3-dependent inflammasome function, but it is not known whether ASC is a sufficient partner and whether inflammasome formation occurs in the cytosol or in association with mitochondria is controversial. Here, we show that the mitochondria-associated adaptor molecule, MAVS, is required for optimal NLRP3 inflammasome activity. MAVS mediates recruitment of NLRP3 to mitochondria, promoting production of IL-1beta and the pathophysiologic activity of the NLRP3 inflammasome in vivo. Our data support a more complex model of NLRP3 inflammasome activation than previously appreciated, with at least two adapters required for maximal function. Because MAVS is a mitochondria-associated molecule previously considered to be uniquely involved in type 1 interferon production, these findings also reveal unexpected polygamous involvement of PYD/CARD-domain-containing adapters in innate immune signaling events.", citationTitle:"Cell. 2013 Apr 11;153(2):348-61. doi: 10.1016/j.cell.2013.02.054.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"23582325", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23582325"}}, {start: {_id:1981}, end: {_id:1634}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"NLRP3 is a key component of the macromolecular signaling complex called the inflammasome that promotes caspase 1-dependent production of IL-1beta. The adaptor ASC is necessary for NLRP3-dependent inflammasome function, but it is not known whether ASC is a sufficient partner and whether inflammasome formation occurs in the cytosol or in association with mitochondria is controversial. Here, we show that the mitochondria-associated adaptor molecule, MAVS, is required for optimal NLRP3 inflammasome activity. MAVS mediates recruitment of NLRP3 to mitochondria, promoting production of IL-1beta and the pathophysiologic activity of the NLRP3 inflammasome in vivo. Our data support a more complex model of NLRP3 inflammasome activation than previously appreciated, with at least two adapters required for maximal function. Because MAVS is a mitochondria-associated molecule previously considered to be uniquely involved in type 1 interferon production, these findings also reveal unexpected polygamous involvement of PYD/CARD-domain-containing adapters in innate immune signaling events.", citationTitle:"Cell. 2013 Apr 11;153(2):348-61. doi: 10.1016/j.cell.2013.02.054.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph", "Interferon signaling subgraph"], pmid:"23582325", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23582325"}}, {start: {_id:1634}, end: {_id:1635}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"NLRP3 is a key component of the macromolecular signaling complex called the inflammasome that promotes caspase 1-dependent production of IL-1beta. The adaptor ASC is necessary for NLRP3-dependent inflammasome function, but it is not known whether ASC is a sufficient partner and whether inflammasome formation occurs in the cytosol or in association with mitochondria is controversial. Here, we show that the mitochondria-associated adaptor molecule, MAVS, is required for optimal NLRP3 inflammasome activity. MAVS mediates recruitment of NLRP3 to mitochondria, promoting production of IL-1beta and the pathophysiologic activity of the NLRP3 inflammasome in vivo. Our data support a more complex model of NLRP3 inflammasome activation than previously appreciated, with at least two adapters required for maximal function. Because MAVS is a mitochondria-associated molecule previously considered to be uniquely involved in type 1 interferon production, these findings also reveal unexpected polygamous involvement of PYD/CARD-domain-containing adapters in innate immune signaling events.", citationTitle:"Cell. 2013 Apr 11;153(2):348-61. doi: 10.1016/j.cell.2013.02.054.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph"], pmid:"23582325", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23582325"}}, {start: {_id:203}, end: {_id:2023}, properties:{evidence:"Here, we show that LRRK2 interacts with human peroxiredoxin 3 (PRDX3), a mitochondrial member of the antioxidant family of thioredoxin (Trx) peroxidases.", citationTitle:"Hum Mutat. 2011 Dec;32(12):1390-7. doi: 10.1002/humu.21582. Epub 2011 Sep 12.", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], pmid:"21850687", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21850687"}}, {start: {_id:463}, end: {_id:203}, properties:{evidence:"Furthermore, we show that protein kinase A (PKA), but not LRRK2 kinase itself, can cause the phosphorylation of LRRK2 at S935 in vitro and in cell culture, suggesting that PKA is a potential upstream kinase that regulates LRRK2 function.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2011 Mar 1;6(3):e17153. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0017153.", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"21390248", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21390248"}}, {start: {_id:203}, end: {_id:386}, properties:{evidence:"Here, using co-immunoprecipitation, we found that LRRK2 interacted with synphilin-1 (SP1), a cytoplasmic protein that interacts with α-synuclein and has implications in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2016 Feb 15;25(4):672-80. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddv504. Epub 2016 Jan 6.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "LRRK2 subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"26744328", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26744328"}}, {start: {_id:386}, end: {_id:137}, properties:{evidence:"Here, using co-immunoprecipitation, we found that LRRK2 interacted with synphilin-1 (SP1), a cytoplasmic protein that interacts with α-synuclein and has implications in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2016 Feb 15;25(4):672-80. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddv504. Epub 2016 Jan 6.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "LRRK2 subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"26744328", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26744328"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
