UNWIND [{start: {_id:238}, end: {_id:250}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:240}, end: {_id:250}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:249}, end: {_id:250}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:300}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that PGAM5 negatively regulates the PINK1 pathway related to maintenance of the mitochondria and, furthermore, that PGAM5 acts between PINK1 and Parkin, or functions independently of Parkin downstream of PINK1.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:326}, end: {_id:325}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, the gracile axonal dystrophy (gad) mouse, which has a naturally deleted ubiquitin carboxy-terminal hydrolase-1 (UCH-L1), is characterized by formation of spheroid bodies in nerve terminals, which are negative for alpha-synuclein but positive for beta-synuclein or gamma-synuclein .", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Axons"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:327}, end: {_id:325}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, the gracile axonal dystrophy (gad) mouse, which has a naturally deleted ubiquitin carboxy-terminal hydrolase-1 (UCH-L1), is characterized by formation of spheroid bodies in nerve terminals, which are negative for alpha-synuclein but positive for beta-synuclein or gamma-synuclein .", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Axons"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:330}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Western blot analysis confirmed the reduction in one of these proteins (HNRNPK), and that its levels were dependent on 26S proteasomal activity.", citationTitle:"Proteomics. 2009 Sep;9(18):4284-97. doi: 10.1002/pmic.200900126.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Pre-translational events"], pmid:"19725078", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19725078"}}, {start: {_id:482}, end: {_id:484}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These studies reveal a predominant role for PP2A, which is the most efficient phosphatase to dephosphorylate tau in vitro at S202, S262, and S356, but not S396 .", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:482}, end: {_id:486}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These studies reveal a predominant role for PP2A, which is the most efficient phosphatase to dephosphorylate tau in vitro at S202, S262, and S356, but not S396 .", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:482}, end: {_id:488}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These studies reveal a predominant role for PP2A, which is the most efficient phosphatase to dephosphorylate tau in vitro at S202, S262, and S356, but not S396 .", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Tau protein subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:482}, end: {_id:441}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"For alpha-syn , targeting PP2A holo-enzymes is a preferred strategy , more specifically through the activation of PP2A to reduce alpha- syn-129 phosphorylation level.", citationTitle:"Front Genet. 2014 Nov 7;5:382. doi: 10.3389/fgene.2014.00382. eCollection 2014", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], pmid:"25426138", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25426138"}}, {start: {_id:529}, end: {_id:534}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"As well as its effects on GSK-3 beta , an additional contribution to Ucn 1-induced neuroprotection, was found to be associated with its ability to directly block the activity of the enzyme Histone deacetylase 3 (HDAC 3) in neuronal cells.", citationTitle:"Int J Biochem Cell Biol. 2015 Mar;60C:130-138. doi: 10.1016/j.biocel.2014.12.005. Epub 2014 Dec 23.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph", "Transcription subgraph"], annotationCell:["neuron"], pmid:"25541373", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25541373"}}, {start: {_id:617}, end: {_id:621}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"DJ-1 knockout astrocytes showed blunted ability to increase the expression of cellular protective mechanisms against oxidative stress mediated via Nrf-2 and HO-1 in response to exposure to 6-OHDA.", citationTitle:"Journal of molecular neuroscience : MN; Vol. 50; Iss. 3", annotationSubgraph:["Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Astrocytes"], pmid:"23536331", annotationDisease:["neuroblastoma"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23536331"}}, {start: {_id:617}, end: {_id:628}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"DJ-1 knockout astrocytes showed blunted ability to increase the expression of cellular protective mechanisms against oxidative stress mediated via Nrf-2 and HO-1 in response to exposure to 6-OHDA.", citationTitle:"Journal of molecular neuroscience : MN; Vol. 50; Iss. 3", annotationSubgraph:["Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Astrocytes"], pmid:"23536331", annotationDisease:["neuroblastoma"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23536331"}}, {start: {_id:629}, end: {_id:630}, properties:{evidence:"Although the densities of VMAT2 and D1 receptor were unchanged in Pink1 knockout, but both were increased in DJ-1 knockout rats.", citationTitle:"Neuroscience letters; Vol. 557 Pt B; Iss.", annotationSpecies:["10116"], pmid:"24157858", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24157858"}}, {start: {_id:629}, end: {_id:631}, properties:{evidence:"Although the densities of VMAT2 and D1 receptor were unchanged in Pink1 knockout, but both were increased in DJ-1 knockout rats.", citationTitle:"Neuroscience letters; Vol. 557 Pt B; Iss.", annotationSpecies:["10116"], pmid:"24157858", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24157858"}}, {start: {_id:617}, end: {_id:632}, properties:{evidence:"The densities of D2 and D3 receptors, determined by mathematical analysis of binding of radioligands [(3)H]WC-10 and [(3)H]raclopride, were significantly increased in both knockout models.", citationTitle:"Neuroscience letters; Vol. 557 Pt B; Iss.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "Dopaminergic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], pmid:"2