UNWIND [{start: {_id:24}, end: {_id:26}, properties:{evidence:"Sequence analysis identified eight genes (IGF2R, SLC22A1, SLC22A2, SLC22A3, PLG, LPA, MAP3K4, and PARK2) as mapping within the large FRA6E region. PARK2, the gene associated with autosomal recessive juvenile parkinsonism (ARJP), accounts for more than half of the CFS. Homozygous deletions and large heterozygous deletions have been observed in PARK2 in ARJP patients.", citationTitle:"Genes Chromosomes Cancer. 2003 Sep;38(1):40-52.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"12874785", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12874785"}}, {start: {_id:18}, end: {_id:18}, properties:{evidence:"Characterization of FRA6E(6q26), the third most frequently observed common fragile site (CFS) in the human population, determined that aphidicolin-induced instability at FRA6E extends over a very large region (3.6 Mb)", citationTitle:"Genes Chromosomes Cancer. 2003 Sep;38(1):40-52.", annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"12874785", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12874785"}}, {start: {_id:1193}, end: {_id:1194}, properties:{evidence:"Twenty-two patients were positive for the GAG deletion in the DYT1 gene. Fifteen of them presented with the typical DYT1 phenotype (early, limb-onset generalized dystonia without spread to craniocervical muscles), four had limb-onset dystonia with spread to craniocervical muscles, two patients had arm-onset segmental dystonia and one had focal right-arm dystonia.", citationTitle:"Brain. 1998 Dec;121 ( Pt 12):2335-9. ", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Dystonia"], pmid:"9874484", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"9874484"}}, {start: {_id:1197}, end: {_id:1194}, properties:{evidence:"Although the c.904_906delGAG mutation in Exon 5 of TOR1A typically manifests as early-onset generalized dystonia, DYT1 dystonia is genetically and clinically heterogeneous. Recently, another Exon 5 mutation (c.863G>A) has been associated with early-onset generalized dystonia", citationTitle:"BMC Med Genet. 2009 Mar 11;10:24.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Dystonia"], pmid:"19284587", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19284587"}}, {start: {_id:1199}, end: {_id:1194}, properties:{evidence:"Although the c.904_906delGAG mutation in Exon 5 of TOR1A typically manifests as early-onset generalized dystonia, DYT1 dystonia is genetically and clinically heterogeneous. Recently, another Exon 5 mutation (c.863G>A) has been associated with early-onset generalized dystonia", citationTitle:"BMC Med Genet. 2009 Mar 11;10:24.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Dystonia"], pmid:"19284587", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19284587"}}, {start: {_id:1235}, end: {_id:1234}, properties:{evidence:"By contrast, Dias and Resslers work with acetophenone takes advantage of specific biology: the chemical binds to a particular receptor in the nose that is encoded by a single gene, dubbed Olfr151. Somehow, the information about the frightening smell gets into a mouse's testes and results in lower methylation of the Olfr151 gene in sperm DNA.", citationTitle:"nature:News feature", annotationSubgraph:["G-protein-mediated signaling"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], citationType:"Online Resource", citationRef:"7490"}}, {start: {_id:1243}, end: {_id:1242}, properties:{annotationPdstage:["Sporadic_PD"], evidence:"We found decreased methylation of the cytochrome P450 2E1 gene and increased expression of CYP2E1 messenger RNA in PD patients' brains, suggesting that epigenetic variants of this cytochrome contribute to PD susceptibility.", citationTitle:"Neurogenetics. 2012 Feb;13(1):87-91. doi: 10.1007/s10048-011-0308-3. Epub 2012 Jan 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Cholesterol metabolism subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], pmid:"22238121", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22238121"}}, {start: {_id:1290}, end: {_id:1291}, properties:{evidence:"Genotyping for MTHFR polymorphisms was carried out by DG-DGGE.The TT+AA genotype was significantly more frequent in PD patients than in controls", citationTitle:"Neuromolecular Med. 2007;9(3):249-54.", annotationSubgraph:["Folate metabolic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17914182", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17914182"}}, {start: {_id:1303}, end: {_id:1304}, properties:{annotationPdstage:["Familial_autosomal_recessive_PD"], evidence:"The authors performed PINK1 mutation analysis of 51 families with autosomal recessive Parkinson disease (ARPD). They found two novel PINK1 mutations: one was a homozygous deletion (13516-18118del) and the other a homozygous missense mutation (C388R). Clinically, the patients with the deletion had dementia. Thus, early-onset PD with dementia may be considered PINK1-linked parkinsonism.", citationTitle:"Neurology. 2005 Jun 14;64(11):1955-7.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Dementia", "Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"15955953", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15955953"}}, {start: {_id:1310}, end: {_id:1313}, properties:{evidence:"We identified two mutations in NR4A2 associated with Parkinson disease (-291Tdel and -245T-->G), ,which map to the first exon of NR4A2 and affect one allele in 10 of 107 individuals with familial Parkinson disease but not in any individuals with sporadic Parkinson disease (n = 94) or in unaffected controls (n = 221). The age at onset of disease and clinical features of these ten individuals were not different from those of individuals with typical Parkinson disease.", citationTitle:"Nat Genet. 2003 Jan;33(1):85-9. Epub 2002 Dec 23.", annotationSubgraph:["Transcription subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"12496759", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12496759"}}, {start: {_id:1321}, end: {_id:1320}, properties:{evidence:"Additionally, we investigated the methylation of SEPW1 and PRKAR2A regulatory regions because both genes were found to be up-regulated in PD brains in a comprehensive microarray study and are expected to be hypomethylated. We found significant decreases in methylation for SEPW1 only in the DLB group, whereas PRKAR2A promoter was hypomethylated only in PD samples, indicating that specific genes might be dysregulated in each disorder. Taken together, this evidence suggests that mislocalization of Dnmt1 and reduction of DNA methylation might directly affect gene expression.", citationTitle:"J Biol Chem. 2011 Mar 18;286(11):9031-7. doi: 10.1074/jbc.C110.212589. Epub 2011 Feb 4", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph", "Transport related subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"21296890", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21296890"}}, {start: {_id:1252}, end: {_id:1252}, properties:{evidence:"we identified methylation and expression changes associated with PD risk variants in PARK16/1q32, GPNMB/7p15, and STX1B/16p11 loci, hence suggesting potential molecular mechanisms and candidate genes at these risk loci.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2011 Jun;7(6):e1002142. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1002142. Epub 2011 Jun 30.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"21738488", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21738488"}}, {start: {_id:1327}, end: {_id:1327}, properties:{evidence:"we identified methylation and expression changes associated with PD risk variants in PARK16/1q32, GPNMB/7p15, and STX1B/16p11 loci, hence suggesting potential molecular mechanisms and candidate genes at these risk loci.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2011 Jun;7(6):e1002142. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1002142. Epub 2011 Jun 30.", annotationSubgraph:["Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"21738488", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21738488"}}, {start: {_id:1328}, end: {_id:1328}, properties:{evidence:"we identified methylation and expression changes associated with PD risk variants in PARK16/1q32, GPNMB/7p15, and STX1B/16p11 loci, hence suggesting potential molecular mechanisms and candidate genes at these risk loci.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2011 Jun;7(6):e1002142. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1002142. Epub 2011 Jun 30.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"21738488", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21738488"}}, {start: {_id:1375}, end: {_id:1304}, properties:{evidence:"We have identified two homozygous mutations affecting the PINK1 kinase domain in three consanguineous PARK6 families: a truncating nonsense mutation and a missense mutation at a highly conserved amino acid.The Spanish family carried a G->A transition in exon 4,resulting in amino acid substitution(G309D)at highly conserved position.Both Italian families carried the same G->A transition in exon 7 which results in W437OPA substitution.", citationTitle:"Science. 2004 May 21;304(5674):1158-60. Epub 2004 Apr 15.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"15087508", annotationTranscriptionallyActiveRegion:["exon 4"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15087508", annotationRace:["Spanish"]}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:IS_A]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1859}, end: {_id:1838}, properties:{}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:HAS__COMPLEX]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:267}, end: {_id:1932}, properties:{evidence:"It is possible that ROS produced by glutamate excitotoxicity could oxidatively modify some of the cytosolic Parkin pool, reducing the translocation to mitochondria", citationTitle:"Neurobiol Dis. 2015 Feb;74:180-93. doi: 10.1016/j.nbd.2014.11.015. Epub 2014 Dec 3.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25478815", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25478815"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:DECREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:234}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{evidence:"PINK1 mutants associated with recessive Parkinson's disease are defective in inhibiting mitochondrial release of cytochrome c.Mutations in PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause recessive familial type 6 of Parkinson's disease (PARK6). We investigated molecular mechanisms underlying PINK1 neuroprotective function and PARK6 mutation-induced loss of PINK1 function. Overexpression of wild- type PINK1 blocked mitochondrial release of apoptogenic cytochrome c, caspase-3 activation and apoptotic cell death induced by proteasome inhibitor MG132. N-terminal truncated PINK1 (NDelta35), which lacks mitochondrial localization sequence, did not block MG132-induced cytochrome c release and cytotoxicity. Despite mitochondrial expression, PARK6 mutant (E240K), (H271Q), (G309D), (L347P), (E417G) and C-terminal truncated (CDelta145) PINK1 failed to inhibit MG132-induced cytochrome c release and caspase-3 activation. Overexpression of wild-type PINK1 blocked cytochrome c release and cell death caused by atractyloside, which opens mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). PARK6 PINK1 mutants failed to inhibit atractyloside-induced cytochrome c release. These results suggest that PINK1 exerts anti-apoptotic effect by inhibiting the opening of mPTP and that PARK6 mutant PINK1 loses its ability to prevent mPTP opening and cytochrome c release.", citationTitle:"Neurobiol Dis. 2007 Nov;28(2):216-26. Epub 2007 Jul 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17707122", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17707122"}}, {start: {_id:236}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{evidence:"PINK1 mutants associated with recessive Parkinson's disease are defective in inhibiting mitochondrial release of cytochrome c.Mutations in PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause recessive familial type 6 of Parkinson's disease (PARK6). We investigated molecular mechanisms underlying PINK1 neuroprotective function and PARK6 mutation-induced loss of PINK1 function. Overexpression of wild- type PINK1 blocked mitochondrial release of apoptogenic cytochrome c, caspase-3 activation and apoptotic cell death induced by proteasome inhibitor MG132. N-terminal truncated PINK1 (NDelta35), which lacks mitochondrial localization sequence, did not block MG132-induced cytochrome c release and cytotoxicity. Despite mitochondrial expression, PARK6 mutant (E240K), (H271Q), (G309D), (L347P), (E417G) and C-terminal truncated (CDelta145) PINK1 failed to inhibit MG132-induced cytochrome c release and caspase-3 activation. Overexpression of wild-type PINK1 blocked cytochrome c release and cell death caused by atractyloside, which opens mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). PARK6 PINK1 mutants failed to inhibit atractyloside-induced cytochrome c release. These results suggest that PINK1 exerts anti-apoptotic effect by inhibiting the opening of mPTP and that PARK6 mutant PINK1 loses its ability to prevent mPTP opening and cytochrome c release.", citationTitle:"Neurobiol Dis. 2007 Nov;28(2):216-26. Epub 2007 Jul 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17707122", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17707122"}}, {start: {_id:238}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{evidence:"PINK1 mutants associated with recessive Parkinson's disease are defective in inhibiting mitochondrial release of cytochrome c.Mutations in PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause recessive familial type 6 of Parkinson's disease (PARK6). We investigated molecular mechanisms underlying PINK1 neuroprotective function and PARK6 mutation-induced loss of PINK1 function. Overexpression of wild- type PINK1 blocked mitochondrial release of apoptogenic cytochrome c, caspase-3 activation and apoptotic cell death induced by proteasome inhibitor MG132. N-terminal truncated PINK1 (NDelta35), which lacks mitochondrial localization sequence, did not block MG132-induced cytochrome c release and cytotoxicity. Despite mitochondrial expression, PARK6 mutant (E240K), (H271Q), (G309D), (L347P), (E417G) and C-terminal truncated (CDelta145) PINK1 failed to inhibit MG132-induced cytochrome c release and caspase-3 activation. Overexpression of wild-type PINK1 blocked cytochrome c release and cell death caused by atractyloside, which opens mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). PARK6 PINK1 mutants failed to inhibit atractyloside-induced cytochrome c release. These results suggest that PINK1 exerts anti-apoptotic effect by inhibiting the opening of mPTP and that PARK6 mutant PINK1 loses its ability to prevent mPTP opening and cytochrome c release.", citationTitle:"Neurobiol Dis. 2007 Nov;28(2):216-26. Epub 2007 Jul 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17707122", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17707122"}}, {start: {_id:240}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{evidence:"PINK1 mutants associated with recessive Parkinson's disease are defective in inhibiting mitochondrial release of cytochrome c.Mutations in PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause recessive familial type 6 of Parkinson's disease (PARK6). We investigated molecular mechanisms underlying PINK1 neuroprotective function and PARK6 mutation-induced loss of PINK1 function. Overexpression of wild- type PINK1 blocked mitochondrial release of apoptogenic cytochrome c, caspase-3 activation and apoptotic cell death induced by proteasome inhibitor MG132. N-terminal truncated PINK1 (NDelta35), which lacks mitochondrial localization sequence, did not block MG132-induced cytochrome c release and cytotoxicity. Despite mitochondrial expression, PARK6 mutant (E240K), (H271Q), (G309D), (L347P), (E417G) and C-terminal truncated (CDelta145) PINK1 failed to inhibit MG132-induced cytochrome c release and caspase-3 activation. Overexpression of wild-type PINK1 blocked cytochrome c release and cell death caused by atractyloside, which opens mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). PARK6 PINK1 mutants failed to inhibit atractyloside-induced cytochrome c release. These results suggest that PINK1 exerts anti-apoptotic effect by inhibiting the opening of mPTP and that PARK6 mutant PINK1 loses its ability to prevent mPTP opening and cytochrome c release.", citationTitle:"Neurobiol Dis. 2007 Nov;28(2):216-26. Epub 2007 Jul 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17707122", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17707122"}}, {start: {_id:242}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{evidence:"PINK1 mutants associated with recessive Parkinson's disease are defective in inhibiting mitochondrial release of cytochrome c.Mutations in PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause recessive familial type 6 of Parkinson's disease (PARK6). We investigated molecular mechanisms underlying PINK1 neuroprotective function and PARK6 mutation-induced loss of PINK1 function. Overexpression of wild- type PINK1 blocked mitochondrial release of apoptogenic cytochrome c, caspase-3 activation and apoptotic cell death induced by proteasome inhibitor MG132. N-terminal truncated PINK1 (NDelta35), which lacks mitochondrial localization sequence, did not block MG132-induced cytochrome c release and cytotoxicity. Despite mitochondrial expression, PARK6 mutant (E240K), (H271Q), (G309D), (L347P), (E417G) and C-terminal truncated (CDelta145) PINK1 failed to inhibit MG132-induced cytochrome c release and caspase-3 activation. Overexpression of wild-type PINK1 blocked cytochrome c release and cell death caused by atractyloside, which opens mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP). PARK6 PINK1 mutants failed to inhibit atractyloside-induced cytochrome c release. These results suggest that PINK1 exerts anti-apoptotic effect by inhibiting the opening of mPTP and that PARK6 mutant PINK1 loses its ability to prevent mPTP opening and cytochrome c release.", citationTitle:"Neurobiol Dis. 2007 Nov;28(2):216-26. Epub 2007 Jul 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17707122", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17707122"}}, {start: {_id:238}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:240}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:249}, end: {_id:230}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:617}, end: {_id:624}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Overexpression of DJ-1 results in increased Nrf2 protein levels, promotes its translocation into the nucleus and enhances its recruitment onto the ARE site in the Trx1 promoter.", citationTitle:"Human molecular genetics; Vol. 21; Iss. 13", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], pmid:"22492997", annotationCellline:["SH-SY5Y cell"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22492997"}}, {start: {_id:1436}, end: {_id:1435}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Consistent with this, Parkin translocation to mitochondria was enhanced by BH3-only proteins or a BH3-only mimetic. Taken together with their role as regulators of apoptosis-associated mitochondrial permeabilization, as well as mitochondrial fission/fusion dynamics, this suggests that Bcl-2 family proteins act as global regulators of mitochondrial homeostasis.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2014 Aug 7;55(3):451-66. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2014.06.001. Epub 2014 Jul 3.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], pmid:"24999239", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24999239"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1450}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 kinase activity and its mitochondrial localization sequence are prerequisites to induce translocation of the E3 ligase Parkin to depolarized mitochondria.", citationTitle:"Nat Cell Biol. 2010 Feb;12(2):119-31. doi: 10.1038/ncb2012. Epub 2010 Jan 24.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"20098416", annotationCellline:["HeLa cell"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20098416"}}, {start: {_id:1465}, end: {_id:1450}, properties:{evidence:"We found that although mutation of T175 and T217 to alanine blocked recruitment of Parkin to mitochondria, as was reported previously, the phosphomimetic mutants T175E, T217E, and T175, 217E do not translocate to mitochondria spontaneously.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2010 Jan 26;8(1):e1000298. doi: 10.1371/journal.pbio.1000298.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20126261", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20126261"}}, {start: {_id:1467}, end: {_id:1450}, properties:{evidence:"We found that although mutation of T175 and T217 to alanine blocked recruitment of Parkin to mitochondria, as was reported previously, the phosphomimetic mutants T175E, T217E, and T175, 217E do not translocate to mitochondria spontaneously.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2010 Jan 26;8(1):e1000298. doi: 10.1371/journal.pbio.1000298.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20126261", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20126261"}}, {start: {_id:1511}, end: {_id:1491}, properties:{evidence:"We found that the overexpression of Î”OTC induces PINK1 accumulation, PARK2 translocation, mitophagy and the reduction of unfolded proteins.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1750-7. doi: 10.4161/auto.26122. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24149988", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149988"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1435}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Mutations in PTEN-induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) or parkin cause autosomal recessive forms of Parkinson's disease.Recent work suggests that loss of mitochondrial membrane potential stabilizes PINK1 and that accumulated PINK1 recruits parkin from the cytoplasm to mitochondria for elimination of depolarized mitochondria, which is known as mitophagy.In this study, we find that PINK1 forms a complex with sterile ÃŽÂ± and TIR motif containing 1 (SARM1) and tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor 6 (TRAF6), which is important for import of PINK1 in the outer membrane and stabilization of PINK1 on depolarized mitochondria. SARM1, which is known to be an adaptor protein for Toll-like receptor, binds to PINK1 and promotes TRAF6-mediated lysine 63 chain ubiquitination of PINK1 at lysine 433. Down-regulation of SARM1 and TRAF6 abrogates accumulation of PINK1, followed by recruitment of parkin to damaged mitochondria. Some pathogenic mutations of PINK1 reduce the complex formation and ubiquitination.These results indicate that association of PINK1 with SARM1 and TRAF6 is an important step for mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Mol Biol Cell. 2013 Sep;24(18):2772-84. doi: 10.1091/mbc.E13-01-0016. Epub 2013 Jul 24.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"23885119", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23885119"}}, {start: {_id:1553}, end: {_id:1859}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Tom20/ TOM may act in the initial recruitment of nRCC mRNA RNPs from the cytosol to MOM, analogous to the targeting of ER-associated mRNAs, stalled ribosomes, and the SRP to the ER.", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:634}, end: {_id:1618}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"BACKGROUND:PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1) is linked to recessive Parkinsonism (EOPD). Pink1 deletion results in impaired dopamine (DA) release and decreased mitochondrial respiration in the striatum of mice. To reveal additional mechanisms of Pink1-related dopaminergic dysfunction, we studied CaÃ‚Â²+ vulnerability of purified brain mitochondria, DA levels and metabolism and whether signaling pathways implicated in Parkinson's disease (PD) display altered activity in the nigrostriatal system of Pink1Ã¢ï¿½Â»/Ã¢ï¿½Â» mice.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2011 Jan 13;6(1):e16038. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0016038.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"21249202", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21249202"}}, {start: {_id:1462}, end: {_id:1977}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In cells with knocked-down VDAC1, caspase-1 activation and IL-1b secretion were considerably impaired for all inflammasome activators examined. As a consequence of VDAC1 knockdown, mitochondrial ROS was highly diminished . In contrast to the NLRP3 inflammasome, VDAC1 was not essential for the activation of the IPAF or the AIM2 inflammasome. A significant reduction of IL-1b secretion was also seen in cells where VDAC2 was knocked down .", citationTitle:"Nat Immunol. 2011 Mar;12(3):222-30. doi: 10.1038/ni.1980. Epub 2010 Dec 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph"], pmid:"21151103", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151103"}}, {start: {_id:1984}, end: {_id:1977}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In cells with knocked-down VDAC1, caspase-1 activation and IL-1b secretion were considerably impaired for all inflammasome activators examined. As a consequence of VDAC1 knockdown, mitochondrial ROS was highly diminished . In contrast to the NLRP3 inflammasome, VDAC1 was not essential for the activation of the IPAF or the AIM2 inflammasome. A significant reduction of IL-1b secretion was also seen in cells where VDAC2 was knocked down .", citationTitle:"Nat Immunol. 2011 Mar;12(3):222-30. doi: 10.1038/ni.1980. Epub 2010 Dec 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph"], pmid:"21151103", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151103"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1888}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"This direct protein-protein interaction was not affected by point mutations of cysteine residues in CHCHD4 (Figure 1C) that pre- viously have been implicated in its mitochondrial activity", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Mitochondrial subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:335}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Neither AIF nor CHCHD4 affected the abundance of other mitochondrial proteins such as VDAC, PINK, and HSP60 (Figures S3C and S3D). The mitochondrial IMS-operating metalloprotease YME1L (Baker et al., 2012; Jensen and Jasper, 2014) is (one of) the protease(s) that participates in the degradation of CHCHD4 substrates after depletion of AIF.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Pre-translational events"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Neither AIF nor CHCHD4 affected the abundance of other mitochondrial proteins such as VDAC, PINK, and HSP60 (Figures S3C and S3D). The mitochondrial IMS-operating metalloprotease YME1L (Baker et al., 2012; Jensen and Jasper, 2014) is (one of) the protease(s) that participates in the degradation of CHCHD4 substrates after depletion of AIF.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:1462}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Neither AIF nor CHCHD4 affected the abundance of other mitochondrial proteins such as VDAC, PINK, and HSP60 (Figures S3C and S3D). The mitochondrial IMS-operating metalloprotease YME1L (Baker et al., 2012; Jensen and Jasper, 2014) is (one of) the protease(s) that participates in the degradation of CHCHD4 substrates after depletion of AIF.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}] AS row
