UNWIND [{start: {_id:56}, end: {_id:57}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Accumulating evidence indicates that insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF1) acts as a homeostatic modulator for normal brain functionality and synaptic plasticity, and the loss of IGF1 activity caused by diabetes may contribute to cognitive impairment [6]. Most biological functions of IGF1, including its anti-apoptotic function, are mediated by the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF1R).", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2014 Sep 30;9(9):e108559. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0108559. eCollection 2014.", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"25268761", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25268761"}}, {start: {_id:56}, end: {_id:55}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Accumulating evidence indicates that insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF1) acts as a homeostatic modulator for normal brain functionality and synaptic plasticity, and the loss of IGF1 activity caused by diabetes may contribute to cognitive impairment [6]. Most biological functions of IGF1, including its anti-apoptotic function, are mediated by the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF1R).", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2014 Sep 30;9(9):e108559. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0108559. eCollection 2014.", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"25268761", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25268761"}}, {start: {_id:108}, end: {_id:103}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"we show that Mind bomb (Mib), a ubiquitin ligase that promotes Notch ligand trafficking and activity, is a crucial molecular substrate for PAR-1. The phosphorylation of Mib by PAR-1 results in Mib degradation, repression of Notch signaling, and stimulation of neuronal differentiation.", citationTitle:"Dev Cell. 2009 Aug;17(2):222-33. doi: 10.1016/j.devcel.2009.06.010.", annotationSubgraph:["G-protein-mediated signaling", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"19686683", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19686683"}}, {start: {_id:130}, end: {_id:131}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Calcium channel voltage-dependent beta-2 subunit (CACNB2) (904) allows calcium influx, participating on the activation of NADPH oxidase, adhesion, degranulation and synthesis of IL-8", citationTitle:"Protein Pept Lett. 2012 Jun 1;19(6):663-72.", annotationSubgraph:["Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "Calcium-dependent signal transduction"], pmid:"22519539", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22519539"}}, {start: {_id:130}, end: {_id:134}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Calcium channel voltage-dependent beta-2 subunit (CACNB2) (904) allows calcium influx, participating on the activation of NADPH oxidase, adhesion, degranulation and synthesis of IL-8", citationTitle:"Protein Pept Lett. 2012 Jun 1;19(6):663-72.", annotationSubgraph:["Calcium-dependent signal transduction"], pmid:"22519539", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22519539"}}, {start: {_id:130}, end: {_id:135}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Calcium channel voltage-dependent beta-2 subunit (CACNB2) (904) allows calcium influx, participating on the activation of NADPH oxidase, adhesion, degranulation and synthesis of IL-8", citationTitle:"Protein Pept Lett. 2012 Jun 1;19(6):663-72.", annotationSubgraph:["Calcium-dependent signal transduction"], pmid:"22519539", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22519539"}}, {start: {_id:215}, end: {_id:62}, properties:{evidence:"In transfection studies, we demonstrated that both the wild type and Gly2385Arg variant LRRK2 protein localize to the cytoplasm and form aggregates. However, under condition of oxidative stress, the Gly2385Arg variant was more toxic and associated with a higher rate of apoptosis. Our study lends support to the contention that the Gly2385Arg is a common risk factor for PD in the Chinese population. Our bioinformatics and in-vitro studies also suggest that the Gly2385Arg variant is biologically relevant and it might act through pro-apoptotic mechanisms.", citationTitle:"Hum Genet. 2007 Feb;120(6):857-63. Epub 2006 Sep 30.", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"17019612", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17019612"}}, {start: {_id:238}, end: {_id:222}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Response to oxidative stress"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:240}, end: {_id:222}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Response to oxidative stress"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:249}, end: {_id:222}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 protects against oxidative stress by phosphorylating mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1. Mutations in the PTEN induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) gene cause an autosomal recessive form of Parkinson disease (PD). So far, no substrates of PINK1 have been reported, and the mechanism by which PINK1 mutations lead to neurodegeneration is unknown. Here we report the identification of TNF receptor-associated protein 1 (TRAP1), a mitochondrial molecular chaperone also known as heat shock protein 75 (Hsp75), as a cellular substrate for PINK1 kinase. PINK1 binds and colocalizes with TRAP1 in the mitochondria and phosphorylates TRAP1 both in vitro and in vivo. We show that PINK1 protects against oxidative-stress-induced cell death by suppressing cytochrome c release from mitochondria, and this protective action of PINK1 depends on its kinase activity to phosphorylate TRAP1. Moreover, we find that the ability of PINK1 to promote TRAP1 phosphorylation and cell survival is impaired by PD-linked PINK1 G309D, L347P, and W437X mutations. Our findings suggest a novel pathway by which PINK1 phosphorylates downstream effector TRAP1 to prevent oxidative-stress- induced apoptosis and implicate the dysregulation of this Mitochondrial subgraph in PD pathogenesis.", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2007 Jul;5(7):e172. Epub 2007 Jun 19.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Response to oxidative stress"], pmid:"17579517", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17579517"}}, {start: {_id:298}, end: {_id:299}, properties:{evidence:"PTEN-induced kinase 1 (PINK1), which is required for mitochondrial homeostasis, is a gene product responsible for early-onset Parkinson's disease (PD).", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:300}, end: {_id:303}, properties:{evidence:"Here we show that a mitochondrial protein, phosphoglycerate mutase 5 (PGAM5, Gene ID: 192111), which was previously reported to be localized at the outer mitochondrial membrane and to lack a phosphoglycerate mutase activity [17], [18], is involved in the PINK1 pathway, and that loss of PGAM5 activity improves mitochondrial defects caused by PINK1 inactivation in Drosophila.Insulin substrate-4 (IRS-4, GeneID: 8471) and PGAM5 (Figure 1A).", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:300}, end: {_id:311}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggested that dPGAM5 is likely to promote the mitochondrial fission process in Drosophila.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:312}, end: {_id:313}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Evolutionarily-conserved GTPases Mfn and OPA1 promote the mitochondrial fusion event while another GTPase Drp1 regulates the mitochondrial fission.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondria fusion subgraph"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:314}, end: {_id:313}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Evolutionarily-conserved GTPases Mfn and OPA1 promote the mitochondrial fusion event while another GTPase Drp1 regulates the mitochondrial fission.", citationTitle:"PLoS Genet. 2010 Dec 2;6(12):e1001229. doi: 10.1371/journal.pgen.1001229. ", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondria fusion subgraph"], pmid:"21151955", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21151955"}}, {start: {_id:203}, end: {_id:319}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Aggregation of alpha-synuclein may play a primary role in the pathogenesis of sporadic PD .In this regard, Hsu and colleagues were the first to show that accumulation of alpha-synuclein could lead to mitochondrial changes that exacerbate oxidative stress and neurodegeneration: overexpression of alpha-synuclein in hypothalamic neuronal cells resulted in formation of alpha-synuclein-immunopositive inclusion-like structures and mitochondrial changes accompanied by increased levels of free radicals, all of which were ameliorated by pretreatment with antioxidants such as Vitamin E.Evidence has accumulated to suggest that LRRK2 plays a crucial role in the pathology of both familial and sporadic alpha-synuclein -synucleinopathies.Both alpha-synuclein and LRRK2 (PARK8) are involved in various pathologies such as impairment of cytoskeleton dynamics and dysregulation of the protein degradation system [19].", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:319}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Aggregation of alpha-synuclein may play a primary role in the pathogenesis of sporadic PD .In this regard, Hsu and colleagues were the first to show that accumulation of alpha-synuclein could lead to mitochondrial changes that exacerbate oxidative stress and neurodegeneration: overexpression of alpha-synuclein in hypothalamic neuronal cells resulted in formation of alpha-synuclein-immunopositive inclusion-like structures and mitochondrial changes accompanied by increased levels of free radicals, all of which were ameliorated by pretreatment with antioxidants such as Vitamin E.Evidence has accumulated to suggest that LRRK2 plays a crucial role in the pathology of both familial and sporadic alpha-synuclein -synucleinopathies.Both alpha-synuclein and LRRK2 (PARK8) are involved in various pathologies such as impairment of cytoskeleton dynamics and dysregulation of the protein degradation system [19].", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:320}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Aggregation of alpha-synuclein may play a primary role in the pathogenesis of sporadic PD .In this regard, Hsu and colleagues were the first to show that accumulation of alpha-synuclein could lead to mitochondrial changes that exacerbate oxidative stress and neurodegeneration: overexpression of alpha-synuclein in hypothalamic neuronal cells resulted in formation of alpha-synuclein-immunopositive inclusion-like structures and mitochondrial changes accompanied by increased levels of free radicals, all of which were ameliorated by pretreatment with antioxidants such as Vitamin E.Evidence has accumulated to suggest that LRRK2 plays a crucial role in the pathology of both familial and sporadic alpha-synuclein -synucleinopathies.Both alpha-synuclein and LRRK2 (PARK8) are involved in various pathologies such as impairment of cytoskeleton dynamics and dysregulation of the protein degradation system [19].", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:203}, end: {_id:320}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Aggregation of alpha-synuclein may play a primary role in the pathogenesis of sporadic PD .In this regard, Hsu and colleagues were the first to show that accumulation of alpha-synuclein could lead to mitochondrial changes that exacerbate oxidative stress and neurodegeneration: overexpression of alpha-synuclein in hypothalamic neuronal cells resulted in formation of alpha-synuclein-immunopositive inclusion-like structures and mitochondrial changes accompanied by increased levels of free radicals, all of which were ameliorated by pretreatment with antioxidants such as Vitamin E.Evidence has accumulated to suggest that LRRK2 plays a crucial role in the pathology of both familial and sporadic alpha-synuclein -synucleinopathies.Both alpha-synuclein and LRRK2 (PARK8) are involved in various pathologies such as impairment of cytoskeleton dynamics and dysregulation of the protein degradation system [19].", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["LRRK2 subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:137}, end: {_id:222}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Aggregation of alpha-synuclein may play a primary role in the pathogenesis of sporadic PD .In this regard, Hsu and colleagues were the first to show that accumulation of alpha-synuclein could lead to mitochondrial changes that exacerbate oxidative stress and neurodegeneration: overexpression of alpha-synuclein in hypothalamic neuronal cells resulted in formation of alpha-synuclein-immunopositive inclusion-like structures and mitochondrial changes accompanied by increased levels of free radicals, all of which were ameliorated by pretreatment with antioxidants such as Vitamin E.Evidence has accumulated to suggest that LRRK2 plays a crucial role in the pathology of both familial and sporadic alpha-synuclein -synucleinopathies.Both alpha-synuclein and LRRK2 (PARK8) are involved in various pathologies such as impairment of cytoskeleton dynamics and dysregulation of the protein degradation system [19].", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}] AS row
