vated levels of âˆšÃ‰â‰ˆÎ©âˆšÃ‡Â¬Â±-synuclein in human brain", citationTitle:"Am J Hum Genet. 2008 Feb;82(2):283-9. doi: 10.1016/j.ajhg.2007.09.021. Epub 2008 Jan 31.", annotationSubgraph:["Neurotrophic subgraph", "Synuclein subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"18252210", annotationTranscriptionallyActiveRegion:["exon 3"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18252210"}}, {start: {_id:1370}, end: {_id:1372}, properties:{evidence:"it has also been demonstrated that cocaine induces the MAPK pathway and through MSK1 phosphorylates histone H3 at Ser10", citationTitle:"Funct Integr Genomics. 2011 Dec;11(4):523-37. doi: 10.1007/s10142-011-0246-z. Epub 2011 Sep 4.", annotationSubgraph:["MAPK subgraph", "DNA synthesis"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Neostriatum"], pmid:"21892731", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21892731"}}, {start: {_id:1393}, end: {_id:1394}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Knockdown of PDE4D, but not PDE4B, amplifies isoprenaline-stimulated phosphorylation of the beta2-adrenergic receptor (beta2-AR)", citationTitle:"J Biol Chem. 2005 Sep 30;280(39):33178-89. Epub 2005 Jul 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Cyclic AMP subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], pmid:"16030021", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16030021"}}, {start: {_id:1400}, end: {_id:71}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Meteorin and its homologue meteorin-like (metrnl) are two novel proteins regulating glial cell differentiation in central nervous system. Overexpression of meteorin and metrnl further magnified the phosphorylation of Akt upon insulin stimuli in 3T3-L1 cells.", citationTitle:"Biochem Biophys Res Commun. 2013 Jul 13. pii: S0006-291X(13)01148-0. doi: 10.1016/j.bbrc.2013.07.008", annotationSubgraph:["Neurotrophic subgraph"], pmid:"23860268", annotationCellline:["3T3-L1"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23860268"}}, {start: {_id:1399}, end: {_id:71}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Meteorin and its homologue meteorin-like (metrnl) are two novel proteins regulating glial cell differentiation in central nervous system. Overexpression of meteorin and metrnl further magnified the phosphorylation of Akt upon insulin stimuli in 3T3-L1 cells.", citationTitle:"Biochem Biophys Res Commun. 2013 Jul 13. pii: S0006-291X(13)01148-0. doi: 10.1016/j.bbrc.2013.07.008", annotationSubgraph:["Neurotrophic subgraph"], pmid:"23860268", annotationCellline:["3T3-L1"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23860268"}}, {start: {_id:1418}, end: {_id:1419}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The direct ubiquitination of purified Bax was also observed in vitro following incubation with recombinant parkin. We found that parkin prevented basal and apoptotic stress-induced translocation of Bax to the mitochondria.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2012 Apr 17;109(16):6283-8. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1113248109. Epub 2012 Mar 29.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], pmid:"22460798", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22460798"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1428}, properties:{evidence:"Upon mitochondrial depolarization, the Bcl-2 family member Mcl-1 underwent rapid Parkin- and PINK1-dependent polyubiquitination and degradation, which sensitized toward apoptosis via opening of the Bax/Bak channel.", citationTitle:"Cell Rep. 2014 Nov 20;9(4):1538-53. doi: 10.1016/j.celrep.2014.10.046. Epub 2014 Nov 13.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25456142", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25456142"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1428}, properties:{evidence:"Upon mitochondrial depolarization, the Bcl-2 family member Mcl-1 underwent rapid Parkin- and PINK1-dependent polyubiquitination and degradation, which sensitized toward apoptosis via opening of the Bax/Bak channel.", citationTitle:"Cell Rep. 2014 Nov 20;9(4):1538-53. doi: 10.1016/j.celrep.2014.10.046. Epub 2014 Nov 13.", annotationSubgraph:["Bcl-2 subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"25456142", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25456142"}}, {start: {_id:810}, end: {_id:1416}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Inhibition of Drp1 by overexpression of a dominant-negative mutant counteracts the conversion to a punctiform mitochondrial phenotype, prevents the loss of the mitochondrial membrane potential and the release of cytochrome c, and reveals a reproducible swelling of the organelles. Remarkably, inhibition of Drp1 blocks cell death, implicating mitochondrial fission as an important step in apoptosis.", citationTitle:"Dev Cell. 2001 Oct;1(4):515-25.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph"], pmid:"11703942", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11703942"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{evidence:"The expression of mitochondrial PINK1 is necessary for recruitment of Parkin to mitochondria, we found that moderate overexpression of PINK1 dramatically accelerates the kinetics of Parkin recruitment", citationTitle:"PLoS Biol. 2010 Jan 26;8(1):e1000298. doi: 10.1371/journal.pbio.1000298.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20126261", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20126261"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 on the mitochondrial outer membrane recruits Parkin even in the absence of mitochondrial uncoupling", citationTitle:"J Cell Biol. 2010 Nov 29;191(5):933-42. doi: 10.1083/jcb.201008084.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"21115803", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21115803"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1489}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"TOMM7 functions to recruit parkin to damaged mitochondria by stabilizing PINK1 in neurons expressing tyrosine hydroxylase (TH1)", citationTitle:"Dev Cell. 2012 Feb 14;22(2):320-33. doi: 10.1016/j.devcel.2011.12.014. Epub 2012 Jan 25.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], pmid:"22280891", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22280891"}}, {start: {_id:1489}, end: {_id:375}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"TOMM7 functions to recruit parkin to damaged mitochondria by stabilizing PINK1 in neurons expressing tyrosine hydroxylase (TH1)", citationTitle:"Dev Cell. 2012 Feb 14;22(2):320-33. doi: 10.1016/j.devcel.2011.12.014. Epub 2012 Jan 25.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph", "Dopaminergic subgraph"], pmid:"22280891", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22280891"}}, {start: {_id:1446}, end: {_id:1492}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Whereas PARKIN is recruited to the mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM) upon depolarization via PINK1 action and can ubiquitylate porin, mitofusin and Miro proteins on the MOM, the full repertoire of PARKIN substrates--the PARKIN-dependent ubiquitylome--remains poorly defined.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:1446}, end: {_id:1493}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Whereas PARKIN is recruited to the mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM) upon depolarization via PINK1 action and can ubiquitylate porin, mitofusin and Miro proteins on the MOM, the full repertoire of PARKIN substrates--the PARKIN-dependent ubiquitylome--remains poorly defined.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:1446}, end: {_id:1494}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Whereas PARKIN is recruited to the mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM) upon depolarization via PINK1 action and can ubiquitylate porin, mitofusin and Miro proteins on the MOM, the full repertoire of PARKIN substrates--the PARKIN-dependent ubiquitylome--remains poorly defined.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:1505}, end: {_id:1494}, properties:{evidence:"We explored the role of these regions in MOM recruitment, MFN2 ubiquitylation, substrate binding, and proteasome association using 5 mutants (Figure S6a): 1) Î”UBL, lacking the UBL domain (residues 1â€“80), 2) K27/48R, which blocks ubiquitylation of two regulated diGLY sites in the UBL (Figure 2), 3) S65A and S65E, which block PINK1-dependent phosphorylation of the UBL domain implicated in PARKIN activation in vitro,18 and 4) C431S, removing the active site Cys in RING2. The PD patient mutation PARKINC431F fails to localize to mitochondria in response to depolarization.15,20 Consistent with this, PARKINC431S failed to localize to mitochondria, efficiently poly- ubiquitylate MFN2, or interact robustly with MOM substrates in response to depolarization. In contrast, K27/K48R, S65A, and S65E mutants displayed near wild-type activity for localization, MFN2 ubiquitylation", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:1507}, end: {_id:1494}, properties:{evidence:"We explored the role of these regions in MOM recruitment, MFN2 ubiquitylation, substrate binding, and proteasome association using 5 mutants (Figure S6a): 1) Î”UBL, lacking the UBL domain (residues 1â€“80), 2) K27/48R, which blocks ubiquitylation of two regulated diGLY sites in the UBL (Figure 2), 3) S65A and S65E, which block PINK1-dependent phosphorylation of the UBL domain implicated in PARKIN activation in vitro,18 and 4) C431S, removing the active site Cys in RING2. The PD patient mutation PARKINC431F fails to localize to mitochondria in response to depolarization.15,20 Consistent with this, PARKINC431S failed to localize to mitochondria, efficiently poly- ubiquitylate MFN2, or interact robustly with MOM substrates in response to depolarization. In contrast, K27/K48R, S65A, and S65E mutants displayed near wild-type activity for localization, MFN2 ubiquitylation", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:1508}, end: {_id:1494}, properties:{evidence:"We explored the role of these regions in MOM recruitment, MFN2 ubiquitylation, substrate binding, and proteasome association using 5 mutants (Figure S6a): 1) Î”UBL, lacking the UBL domain (residues 1â€“80), 2) K27/48R, which blocks ubiquitylation of two regulated diGLY sites in the UBL (Figure 2), 3) S65A and S65E, which block PINK1-dependent phosphorylation of the UBL domain implicated in PARKIN activation in vitro,18 and 4) C431S, removing the active site Cys in RING2. The PD patient mutation PARKINC431F fails to localize to mitochondria in response to depolarization.15,20 Consistent with this, PARKINC431S failed to localize to mitochondria, efficiently poly- ubiquitylate MFN2, or interact robustly with MOM substrates in response to depolarization. In contrast, K27/K48R, S65A, and S65E mutants displayed near wild-type activity for localization, MFN2 ubiquitylation", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:1509}, end: {_id:1494}, properties:{evidence:"We explored the role of these regions in MOM recruitment, MFN2 ubiquitylation, substrate binding, and proteasome association using 5 mutants (Figure S6a): 1) Î”UBL, lacking the UBL domain (residues 1â€“80), 2) K27/48R, which blocks ubiquitylation of two regulated diGLY sites in the UBL (Figure 2), 3) S65A and S65E, which block PINK1-dependent phosphorylation of the UBL domain implicated in PARKIN activation in vitro,18 and 4) C431S, removing the active site Cys in RING2. The PD patient mutation PARKINC431F fails to localize to mitochondria in response to depolarization.15,20 Consistent with this, PARKINC431S failed to localize to mitochondria, efficiently poly- ubiquitylate MFN2, or interact robustly with MOM substrates in response to depolarization. In contrast, K27/K48R, S65A, and S65E mutants displayed near wild-type activity for localization, MFN2 ubiquitylation", citationTitle:"Nature. 2013 Apr 18;496(7445):372-6. doi: 10.1038/nature12043. Epub 2013 Mar 17.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"23503661", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23503661"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"when a mitochondrion loses membrane potential, PINK1 import is blocked and it accumulates on the outer membrane, where it recruits PARK2 specifically to the damaged mitochondrion and activates PARK2 E3 enzyme activity to ubiquitinate mitochondrial substrates and initiate mitophagic clearance.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1750-7. doi: 10.4161/auto.26122. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"24149988", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149988"}}, {start: {_id:1511}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{evidence:"We found that the overexpression of Î”OTC induces PINK1 accumulation, PARK2 translocation, mitophagy and the reduction of unfolded proteins.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1750-7. doi: 10.4161/auto.26122. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24149988", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149988"}}, {start: {_id:1510}, end: {_id:1511}, properties:{evidence:"downregulation of CLPP did not inhibit PINK1 accumulation induced by overexpression of unfolded protein or affect the level of Î”OTC itself, indicating that CLPP is not triggering PINK1 accumulation through peptide generation or Î”OTC degradation. However, the knockdown of another mitochondrial protease, LONP1, robustly increased Î”OTC and PINK1 accumulation, suggesting that Î”OTC is degraded to some extent by LONP1 and that the level of accumulated unfolded protein is important for PINK1 accumulation rather than peptide fragments generated by the protease.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1750-7. doi: 10.4161/auto.26122. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24149988", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149988"}}, {start: {_id:1510}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{evidence:"downregulation of CLPP did not inhibit PINK1 accumulation induced by overexpression of unfolded protein or affect the level of Î”OTC itself, indicating that CLPP is not triggering PINK1 accumulation through peptide generation or Î”OTC degradation. However, the knockdown of another mitochondrial protease, LONP1, robustly increased Î”OTC and PINK1 accumulation, suggesting that Î”OTC is degraded to some extent by LONP1 and that the level of accumulated unfolded protein is important for PINK1 accumulation rather than peptide fragments generated by the protease.", citationTitle:"Autophagy. 2013 Nov 1;9(11):1750-7. doi: 10.4161/auto.26122. Epub 2013 Sep 5.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24149988", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24149988"}}, {start: {_id:1525}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Hexose kinase activity was required for parkin relocalization, suggesting the effects are shared among hexokinases including the brain-expressed hexokinase 1 (HK1). Knockdown of both HK1 and HK2 led to a stronger block in parkin relocalization than either isoform alone, and expression of HK2 in primary neurons promoted YFP-parkin recruitment to depolarized mitochondria. Mitochondrial parkin recruitment was attenuated with AKT inhibition, which is known to modulate HK2 activity and mitochondrial localization. We, therefore, propose that Akt-dependent recruitment of hexokinases is a required step in the recruitment of parkin prior to mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2014 Jan 1;23(1):145-56. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddt407. Epub 2013 Aug 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Energy metabolic subgraph", "Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23962723", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23962723"}}, {start: {_id:1526}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Hexose kinase activity was required for parkin relocalization, suggesting the effects are shared among hexokinases including the brain-expressed hexokinase 1 (HK1). Knockdown of both HK1 and HK2 led to a stronger block in parkin relocalization than either isoform alone, and expression of HK2 in primary neurons promoted YFP-parkin recruitment to depolarized mitochondria. Mitochondrial parkin recruitment was attenuated with AKT inhibition, which is known to modulate HK2 activity and mitochondrial localization. We, therefore, propose that Akt-dependent recruitment of hexokinases is a required step in the recruitment of parkin prior to mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2014 Jan 1;23(1):145-56. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddt407. Epub 2013 Aug 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Energy metabolic subgraph", "Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23962723", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23962723"}}, {start: {_id:1528}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"we identified HK2 as a modifier of parkin recruitment to mitochondria prior to mitophagy in both transformed cells and neurons. Mechanistically, we show that the hexokinase activity of HK2 is critical and PINK1 must be expressed. Furthermore, we establish that Akt contributes to recruitment of parkin via phosphorylation of T473, resulting in recruitment of HK2 to the OMM.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2014 Jan 1;23(1):145-56. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddt407. Epub 2013 Aug 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Energy metabolic subgraph", "Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23962723", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23962723"}}, {start: {_id:404}, end: {_id:1528}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"it is interesting that phosphorylation of T473 of HK2 by Akt has been recently shown to diminish the effects of oxidative stress. we propose that Akt-mediated recruitment of hexokinase to mitochondria is a general cell biological pathway that is of benefit to protect against oxidative stress, which is also a function ascribed to PINK1 both in neurons", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2014 Jan 1;23(1):145-56. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddt407. Epub 2013 Aug 19.", annotationSubgraph:["Energy metabolic subgraph", "Akt/mTOR subgraph"], pmid:"23962723", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23962723"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1459}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 plays a dual role by phosphorylating PARKIN on its UB-like domain and poly-UB chains on mitochondria. PARKIN activation by PINK1 produces canonical and non-canonical UB chains on mitochondria, and PARKIN-dependent chain assembly is required for accumulation of poly- phospho-UB (poly-p-UB) on mitochondria. In vitro, PINK1 directly activates PARKINâ€™s ability to assemble canonical and non-canonical UB chains, and promotes association of PARKIN with both p-UB and poly- p-UB. Our data reveal a feed-forward mechanism that explains how PINK1 phosphorylation of both PARKIN and poly-UB chains synthesized by PARKIN drives a program of PARKIN recruitment and mitochondrial ubiquitylation in response to mitochondrial damage.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2014 Nov 6;56(3):360-75. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2014.09.007. Epub 2014 Oct 2.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25284222", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25284222"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1543}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"here we show that two receptors previously linked to xenophagy, NDP52 and optineurin, are the primary receptors for PINK1- and parkin- mediated mitophagy. PINK1 recruits NDP52 and optineurin, but not p62, to mitochondria to activate mitophagy directly, independently of parkin. Once recruited to mitochondria, NDP52 and optineurin recruit the autophagy factors ULK1, DFCP1 and WIPI1 to focal spots proximal to mitochondria, revealing a function for these autophagy receptors upstream of LC3. This supports a new model in which PINK1-generated phospho-ubiquitin serves as the autophagy signal on mitochondria, and parkin then acts to amplify this signal. This work also suggests direct and broader roles for ubiquitin phosphorylation in other autophagy pathways.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1545}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"here we show that two receptors previously linked to xenophagy, NDP52 and optineurin, are the primary receptors for PINK1- and parkin- mediated mitophagy. PINK1 recruits NDP52 and optineurin, but not p62, to mitochondria to activate mitophagy directly, independently of parkin. Once recruited to mitochondria, NDP52 and optineurin recruit the autophagy factors ULK1, DFCP1 and WIPI1 to focal spots proximal to mitochondria, revealing a function for these autophagy receptors upstream of LC3. This supports a new model in which PINK1-generated phospho-ubiquitin serves as the autophagy signal on mitochondria, and parkin then acts to amplify this signal. This work also suggests direct and broader roles for ubiquitin phosphorylation in other autophagy pathways.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1543}, end: {_id:1546}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"here we show that two receptors previously linked to xenophagy, NDP52 and optineurin, are the primary receptors for PINK1- and parkin- mediated mitophagy. PINK1 recruits NDP52 and optineurin, but not p62, to mitochondria to activate mitophagy directly, independently of parkin. Once recruited to mitochondria, NDP52 and optineurin recruit the autophagy factors ULK1, DFCP1 and WIPI1 to focal spots proximal to mitochondria, revealing a function for these autophagy receptors upstream of LC3. This supports a new model in which PINK1-generated phospho-ubiquitin serves as the autophagy signal on mitochondria, and parkin then acts to amplify this signal. This work also suggests direct and broader roles for ubiquitin phosphorylation in other autophagy pathways.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}, {start: {_id:1543}, end: {_id:1547}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"here we show that two receptors previously linked to xenophagy, NDP52 and optineurin, are the primary receptors for PINK1- and parkin- mediated mitophagy. PINK1 recruits NDP52 and optineurin, but not p62, to mitochondria to activate mitophagy directly, independently of parkin. Once recruited to mitochondria, NDP52 and optineurin recruit the autophagy factors ULK1, DFCP1 and WIPI1 to focal spots proximal to mitochondria, revealing a function for these autophagy receptors upstream of LC3. This supports a new model in which PINK1-generated phospho-ubiquitin serves as the autophagy signal on mitochondria, and parkin then acts to amplify this signal. This work also suggests direct and broader roles for ubiquitin phosphorylation in other autophagy pathways.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}, {start: {_id:1545}, end: {_id:1546}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"here we show that two receptors previously linked to xenophagy, NDP52 and optineurin, are the primary receptors for PINK1- and parkin- mediated mitophagy. PINK1 recruits NDP52 and optineurin, but not p62, to mitochondria to activate mitophagy directly, independently of parkin. Once recruited to mitochondria, NDP52 and optineurin recruit the autophagy factors ULK1, DFCP1 and WIPI1 to focal spots proximal to mitochondria, revealing a function for these autophagy receptors upstream of LC3. This supports a new model in which PINK1-generated phospho-ubiquitin serves as the autophagy signal on mitochondria, and parkin then acts to amplify this signal. This work also suggests direct and broader roles for ubiquitin phosphorylation in other autophagy pathways.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}, {start: {_id:1545}, end: {_id:1547}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"here we show that two receptors previously linked to xenophagy, NDP52 and optineurin, are the primary receptors for PINK1- and parkin- mediated mitophagy. PINK1 recruits NDP52 and optineurin, but not p62, to mitochondria to activate mitophagy directly, independently of parkin. Once recruited to mitochondria, NDP52 and optineurin recruit the autophagy factors ULK1, DFCP1 and WIPI1 to focal spots proximal to mitochondria, revealing a function for these autophagy receptors upstream of LC3. This supports a new model in which PINK1-generated phospho-ubiquitin serves as the autophagy signal on mitochondria, and parkin then acts to amplify this signal. This work also suggests direct and broader roles for ubiquitin phosphorylation in other autophagy pathways.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Apoptosis signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1549}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We propose that PINK1 generates the novel and essential signature (phospho-ubiquitin) on mitochondria to induce OPTN and NDP52 recruitment and mitophagy; parkin acts to increase this signal by generating more ubiquitin chains on mitochondria, which are subsequently phos- phorylated by PINK1. Our findings clarify the role of parkin as an amplifier ofthe PINK1-generated mitophagy signal, phospho-ubiquitin, which can engage the autophagy receptors to recruit ULK1, DFCP1, WIPI1 and LC3", citationTitle:"Nature. 2015 Aug 20;524(7565):309-14. doi: 10.1038/nature14893. Epub 2015 Aug 12.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"26266977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26266977"}}, {start: {_id:1551}, end: {_id:1552}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The TOM complex contains three receptor proteins: Tom20, Tom22, and Tom70.The TOM precursors, including the precursor of Tom22, have to be imported into the mitochondrial outer membrane. CK2 phosphorylates the Tom22 precursor in the cytosol and stimulates its import into mitochondria. Tom20 has been shown to function as receptor for the precursor of Tom22. Phosphorylation of Tom22 by CK2 stimulates its binding to Tom20 and thus promotes its import", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2014 Mar 4;19(3):357-72. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.01.010. Epub 2014 Feb 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24561263", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24561263"}}, {start: {_id:1551}, end: {_id:1553}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"CK2 also promotes the biogenesis of Tom20 and Tom70", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2014 Mar 4;19(3):357-72. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.01.010. Epub 2014 Feb 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24561263", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24561263"}}, {start: {_id:1551}, end: {_id:1518}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"CK2 also promotes the biogenesis of Tom20 and Tom70", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2014 Mar 4;19(3):357-72. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.01.010. Epub 2014 Feb 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph", "Cell cycle subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24561263", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24561263"}}, {start: {_id:1555}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Studies in recent years revealed a crucial role of the mitochondrial protein import machinery for the function of PINK1 and Parkin. PINK1 carries an amino-terminal mitochondrial targeting signal. In healthy cells, PINK1 is partially imported into mitochondria via the TOM and TIM23 complexes in a Î”Ïˆ-dependent manner and proteolytically processed by the inner membrane rhomboid protease PARL (presenilin- associated rhomboid-like protein)", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2014 Mar 4;19(3):357-72. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.01.010. Epub 2014 Feb 20.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Transport related subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24561263", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24561263"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1556}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 phosphorylates mitofusin 2 at the mitochondrial outer membrane, stimulating its function as mitochondrial receptor for Parkin. Mitofusin 2 may thus recruit Parkin to damaged mitochondria.", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2014 Mar 4;19(3):357-72. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.01.010. Epub 2014 Feb 20.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondria fusion subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"24561263", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24561263"}}, {start: {_id:1557}, end: {_id:1558}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We show here that PGC-1, a cold-inducible coactivator of nuclear receptors, stimulates mitochondrial biogenesis and respiration in muscle cells through an induction of uncoupling protein 2 (UCP-2) and through regulation of the nuclear respiratory factors (NRFs). PGC-1 stimulates a powerful induction of NRF-1 and NRF-2 gene expression; in addition, PGC-1 binds to and coactivates the transcriptional function of NRF-1 on the promoter for mitochondrial transcription factor A (mtTFA), a direct regulator of mitochondrial DNA replication/transcription.", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "Transport related subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1557}, end: {_id:1559}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We show here that PGC-1, a cold-inducible coactivator of nuclear receptors, stimulates mitochondrial biogenesis and respiration in muscle cells through an induction of uncoupling protein 2 (UCP-2) and through regulation of the nuclear respiratory factors (NRFs). PGC-1 stimulates a powerful induction of NRF-1 and NRF-2 gene expression; in addition, PGC-1 binds to and coactivates the transcriptional function of NRF-1 on the promoter for mitochondrial transcription factor A (mtTFA), a direct regulator of mitochondrial DNA replication/transcription.", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph", "Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1557}, end: {_id:1560}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We show here that PGC-1, a cold-inducible coactivator of nuclear receptors, stimulates mitochondrial biogenesis and respiration in muscle cells through an induction of uncoupling protein 2 (UCP-2) and through regulation of the nuclear respiratory factors (NRFs). PGC-1 stimulates a powerful induction of NRF-1 and NRF-2 gene expression; in addition, PGC-1 binds to and coactivates the transcriptional function of NRF-1 on the promoter for mitochondrial transcription factor A (mtTFA), a direct regulator of mitochondrial DNA replication/transcription.", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph", "Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1557}, end: {_id:1561}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We show here that PGC-1, a cold-inducible coactivator of nuclear receptors, stimulates mitochondrial biogenesis and respiration in muscle cells through an induction of uncoupling protein 2 (UCP-2) and through regulation of the nuclear respiratory factors (NRFs). PGC-1 stimulates a powerful induction of NRF-1 and NRF-2 gene expression; in addition, PGC-1 binds to and coactivates the transcriptional function of NRF-1 on the promoter for mitochondrial transcription factor A (mtTFA), a direct regulator of mitochondrial DNA replication/transcription.", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph", "Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1560}, end: {_id:1570}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"As shown here, PGC-1 powerfully induces mRNA for NRF-1 and NRF-2Î±, regulators of multiple target genes such as COX IV, Î²-ATP synthetase, and mtTFA. In addition, PGC-1 binds to and coactivates NRF-1, increases its transcriptional activity on target genes, including mtTFA.", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1560}, end: {_id:1561}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"As shown here, PGC-1 powerfully induces mRNA for NRF-1 and NRF-2Î±, regulators of multiple target genes such as COX IV, Î²-ATP synthetase, and mtTFA. In addition, PGC-1 binds to and coactivates NRF-1, increases its transcriptional activity on target genes, including mtTFA.", citationTitle:"Cell. 1999 Jul 9;98(1):115-24.", annotationSubgraph:["Transcription subgraph"], pmid:"10412986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10412986"}}, {start: {_id:1575}, end: {_id:216}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"the hsp-4::gfp reporter, like the endogenous gene, was unaltered by ethidium bromide but was markedly induced by either tunicamycin or heat shock and the cytoplasmic hsp-70::GFP reporter was inducible only by heat shock", citationTitle:"J Cell Sci. 2004 Aug 15;117(Pt 18):4055-66. Epub 2004 Jul 27.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"15280428", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15280428"}}, {start: {_id:1575}, end: {_id:1576}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"the hsp-4::gfp reporter, like the endogenous gene, was unaltered by ethidium bromide but was markedly induced by either tunicamycin or heat shock and the cytoplasmic hsp-70::GFP reporter was inducible only by heat shock", citationTitle:"J Cell Sci. 2004 Aug 15;117(Pt 18):4055-66. Epub 2004 Jul 27.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"15280428", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15280428"}}, {start: {_id:1578}, end: {_id:1579}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Analysis of the chaperonin 60/10 bidirectional promoter showed that the CHOP element was required for the mtUPR and that the transcription of the chop gene is activated by mtUPR. In order to investigate the role of CHOP in the mtUPR, we carried out a deletion analysis of the chop promoter. This revealed that the transcriptional activation of the chop gene by mtUPR is through an AP-1 (activator protein-1) element. This site lies alongside an ERSE element through which chop transcription is activated in response to the ER stress response (erUPR). Thus CHOP can be induced separately in response to 2 different stress response pathways.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2007 Sep 12;2(9):e835.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Unfolded protein response subgraph"], pmid:"17848986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17848986"}}, {start: {_id:1580}, end: {_id:1578}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Analysis of the chaperonin 60/10 bidirectional promoter showed that the CHOP element was required for the mtUPR and that the transcription of the chop gene is activated by mtUPR. In order to investigate the role of CHOP in the mtUPR, we carried out a deletion analysis of the chop promoter. This revealed that the transcriptional activation of the chop gene by mtUPR is through an AP-1 (activator protein-1) element. This site lies alongside an ERSE element through which chop transcription is activated in response to the ER stress response (erUPR). Thus CHOP can be induced separately in response to 2 different stress response pathways.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2007 Sep 12;2(9):e835.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "MAPK subgraph"], pmid:"17848986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17848986"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1581}, end: {_id:1578}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In this paper we describe the identification of an mtUPR response element in the promoters of both chop and c/ebpÎ² genes. This element is an AP-1 site, suggesting that mitochondrial to nuclear signaling of the accumulation of unfolded proteins in the mitochondrial matrix is through a JNK pathway. We show, using a specific MEK inhibitor, that this signaling is through JNK2 and that an inhibition in the phosphorylation of JNK2 also inhibits mtUPR. We suggest that the cell can discriminate between organelle specific Unfolded protein response subgraphs through different pathways to activate genes that harbor different stress elements within their promoters. Recently, it has been reported that JNK2 is a positive regulator of the cJun transcription factor and can regulate both mitochondrial and lysosomal death pathways in mouse embryonic fibroblasts", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2007 Sep 12;2(9):e835.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "MAPK subgraph"], pmid:"17848986", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17848986"}}, {start: {_id:1588}, end: {_id:1589}, properties:{evidence:"Mitochondrial toxins such as paraquat, which activated the UPRmt (Fig. 3A), are known to impair mitochondrial import causing the accumulation of MTS containing proteins in the cytosol . To determine if a general impairment of import occurs during UPRmt activation, we generated a transgenic strain expressing GFP with a MTS (GFPmt). Because steady- state detection of unprocessed MTS containing proteins is very difficult , we expressed GFPmt via the inducible hsp-16 promoter (fig. S6). Only in the presence of UPRmt-activating stress was unprocessed GFPmt detected in the post-mitochondrial supernatant consistent with impaired import (Fig. 2C). Similarly, when ATFS-1 was expressed via the hsp-16 promoter, unprocessed ATFS-1 was only detectable in the postmitochondrial supernatant during UPRmt-activating stress (Figs. 2D & S6). Import was not completely blocked during UPRmt activation because the processed forms of ATFS-1 (revealed by lon(RNAi)) and GFPmt were detected in mitochondria", citationTitle:"Science. 2012 Aug 3;337(6094):587-90. doi: 10.1126/science.1223560. Epub 2012 Jun 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"22700657", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22700657"}}, {start: {_id:1588}, end: {_id:1587}, properties:{evidence:"Mitochondrial toxins such as paraquat, which activated the UPRmt (Fig. 3A), are known to impair mitochondrial import causing the accumulation of MTS containing proteins in the cytosol . To determine if a general impairment of import occurs during UPRmt activation, we generated a transgenic strain expressing GFP with a MTS (GFPmt). Because steady- state detection of unprocessed MTS containing proteins is very difficult , we expressed GFPmt via the inducible hsp-16 promoter (fig. S6). Only in the presence of UPRmt-activating stress was unprocessed GFPmt detected in the post-mitochondrial supernatant consistent with impaired import (Fig. 2C). Similarly, when ATFS-1 was expressed via the hsp-16 promoter, unprocessed ATFS-1 was only detectable in the postmitochondrial supernatant during UPRmt-activating stress (Figs. 2D & S6). Import was not completely blocked during UPRmt activation because the processed forms of ATFS-1 (revealed by lon(RNAi)) and GFPmt were detected in mitochondria", citationTitle:"Science. 2012 Aug 3;337(6094):587-90. doi: 10.1126/science.1223560. Epub 2012 Jun 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"22700657", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22700657"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1555}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Included in the ATFS-1 program were many genes with roles in protecting against mitochondrial dysfunction (Fig. 4C) including the mitochondrial chaperones dnj-10 (14) (Fig. 4D) and hsp-60 (Fig. S13B). Numerous components involved in reactive oxygen species detoxification required ATFS-1 for induction during stress including the transcription factor skn-1 (15) (Fig. 4E). Several glycolysis genes including gpd-2 (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase) were also induced (Fig. 4F) suggesting the UPRmt may contribute to a shift in ATP production from respiration to glycolysis. ATFS-1 was also required for the induction of tim-23 and tim-17 (Figs. 4G & S13C); core components of the TIM23 complex (1).", citationTitle:"Science. 2012 Aug 3;337(6094):587-90. doi: 10.1126/science.1223560. Epub 2012 Jun 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"22700657", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22700657"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1593}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Included in the ATFS-1 program were many genes with roles in protecting against mitochondrial dysfunction (Fig. 4C) including the mitochondrial chaperones dnj-10 (14) (Fig. 4D) and hsp-60 (Fig. S13B). Numerous components involved in reactive oxygen species detoxification required ATFS-1 for induction during stress including the transcription factor skn-1 (15) (Fig. 4E). Several glycolysis genes including gpd-2 (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase) were also induced (Fig. 4F) suggesting the UPRmt may contribute to a shift in ATP production from respiration to glycolysis. ATFS-1 was also required for the induction of tim-23 and tim-17 (Figs. 4G & S13C); core components of the TIM23 complex (1).", citationTitle:"Science. 2012 Aug 3;337(6094):587-90. doi: 10.1126/science.1223560. Epub 2012 Jun 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"22700657", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22700657"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1459}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Here, we report that PINK1 regulates the E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase function of parkin through direct phosphorylation. We find that phosphorylation of parkin by PINK1 activates parkin E3 ligase function for catalyzing K63-linked polyubiquitination and enhances parkin- mediated ubiquitin signaling through the IkappaB kinase/nuclear factor kappaB (NF-kappaB) pathway. Furthermore, the ability of PINK1 to promote parkin phosphorylation and activate parkin-mediated ubiquitin signaling is impaired by PD-linked pathogenic PINK1 mutations. Our findings support a direct link between PINK1-mediated phosphorylation and parkin-mediated ubiquitin signaling and implicate the deregulation of the PINK1/parkin/NF-kappaB neuroprotective signaling pathway in the pathogenesis of PD.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2010 Jan 15;19(2):352-63. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddp501. Epub 2009 Oct 30.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"19880420", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19880420"}}, {start: {_id:1459}, end: {_id:1627}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Here, we report that PINK1 regulates the E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase function of parkin through direct phosphorylation. We find that phosphorylation of parkin by PINK1 activates parkin E3 ligase function for catalyzing K63-linked polyubiquitination and enhances parkin- mediated ubiquitin signaling through the IkappaB kinase/nuclear factor kappaB (NF-kappaB) pathway. Furthermore, the ability of PINK1 to promote parkin phosphorylation and activate parkin-mediated ubiquitin signaling is impaired by PD-linked pathogenic PINK1 mutations. Our findings support a direct link between PINK1-mediated phosphorylation and parkin-mediated ubiquitin signaling and implicate the deregulation of the PINK1/parkin/NF-kappaB neuroprotective signaling pathway in the pathogenesis of PD.", citationTitle:"Hum Mol Genet. 2010 Jan 15;19(2):352-63. doi: 10.1093/hmg/ddp501. Epub 2009 Oct 30.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"19880420", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19880420"}}, {start: {_id:1634}, end: {_id:1142}, properties:{evidence:"Viral infection triggers host innate immune responses through activation of the transcription factors NF-kappaB and IRF 3, which coordinately regulate the expression of type-I interferons such as interferon-beta (IFN-beta). Herein, we report the identification of a novel protein termed MAVS (mitochondrial antiviral signaling), which mediates the activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3 in response to viral infection. Silencing of MAVS expression through RNA interference abolishes the activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3 by viruses, thereby permitting viral replication. Conversely, overexpression of MAVS induces the expression of IFN-beta through activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3, thus boosting antiviral immunity. Epistasis experiments show that MAVS is required for the phosphorylation of IRF 3 and IkappaB and functions downstream of RIG-I, an intracellular receptor for viral RNA. MAVS contains an N-terminal CARD-like domain and a C-terminal transmembrane domain, both of which are essential for MAVS signaling. The transmembrane domain targets MAVS to the mitochondria, implicating a new role of mitochondria in innate immunity.", citationTitle:"Cell. 2005 Sep 9;122(5):669-82.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph", "Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Virus Diseases"], pmid:"16125763", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16125763"}}, {start: {_id:1634}, end: {_id:1635}, properties:{evidence:"Viral infection triggers host innate immune responses through activation of the transcription factors NF-kappaB and IRF 3, which coordinately regulate the expression of type-I interferons such as interferon-beta (IFN-beta). Herein, we report the identification of a novel protein termed MAVS (mitochondrial antiviral signaling), which mediates the activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3 in response to viral infection. Silencing of MAVS expression through RNA interference abolishes the activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3 by viruses, thereby permitting viral replication. Conversely, overexpression of MAVS induces the expression of IFN-beta through activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3, thus boosting antiviral immunity. Epistasis experiments show that MAVS is required for the phosphorylation of IRF 3 and IkappaB and functions downstream of RIG-I, an intracellular receptor for viral RNA. MAVS contains an N-terminal CARD-like domain and a C-terminal transmembrane domain, both of which are essential for MAVS signaling. The transmembrane domain targets MAVS to the mitochondria, implicating a new role of mitochondria in innate immunity.", citationTitle:"Cell. 2005 Sep 9;122(5):669-82.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Virus Diseases"], pmid:"16125763", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16125763"}}, {start: {_id:1634}, end: {_id:1637}, properties:{evidence:"Viral infection triggers host innate immune responses through activation of the transcription factors NF-kappaB and IRF 3, which coordinately regulate the expression of type-I interferons such as interferon-beta (IFN-beta). Herein, we report the identification of a novel protein termed MAVS (mitochondrial antiviral signaling), which mediates the activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3 in response to viral infection. Silencing of MAVS expression through RNA interference abolishes the activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3 by viruses, thereby permitting viral replication. Conversely, overexpression of MAVS induces the expression of IFN-beta through activation of NF-kappaB and IRF 3, thus boosting antiviral immunity. Epistasis experiments show that MAVS is required for the phosphorylation of IRF 3 and IkappaB and functions downstream of RIG-I, an intracellular receptor for viral RNA. MAVS contains an N-terminal CARD-like domain and a C-terminal transmembrane domain, both of which are essential for MAVS signaling. The transmembrane domain targets MAVS to the mitochondria, implicating a new role of mitochondria in innate immunity.", citationTitle:"Cell. 2005 Sep 9;122(5):669-82.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Virus Diseases"], pmid:"16125763", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16125763"}}, {start: {_id:1641}, end: {_id:1642}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Phosphorylation of eIF2Î± induces translational attenuation of new protein synthesis and activates stress-responsive transcription factors such as activating transcription factor 4 (ATF4)", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2013 Dec 3;18(6):908-19. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2013.11.006.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph"], pmid:"24315374", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24315374"}}, {start: {_id:1647}, end: {_id:1648}, properties:{evidence:"P. aeruginosa produces virulence factors that target many cellular functions including the mitochondrial toxins cyanide and pyocyanin15, 16. P. aeruginosa also produces exotoxin A, which impairs protein synthesis and leads to the induction of the innate immune gene irg-1 via the transcription factor ZIP-2 (refs 2, 3, 17).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Virus Diseases"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1647}, end: {_id:1649}, properties:{evidence:"P. aeruginosa produces virulence factors that target many cellular functions including the mitochondrial toxins cyanide and pyocyanin15, 16. P. aeruginosa also produces exotoxin A, which impairs protein synthesis and leads to the induction of the innate immune gene irg-1 via the transcription factor ZIP-2 (refs 2, 3, 17).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Virus Diseases"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1649}, end: {_id:1648}, properties:{evidence:"P. aeruginosa produces virulence factors that target many cellular functions including the mitochondrial toxins cyanide and pyocyanin15, 16. P. aeruginosa also produces exotoxin A, which impairs protein synthesis and leads to the induction of the innate immune gene irg-1 via the transcription factor ZIP-2 (refs 2, 3, 17).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph", "Transport related subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Virus Diseases"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1650}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We examined the role of ATFS-1 in the induction of innate immune genes during P. aeruginosa exposure rather than specifically during mitochondrial stress. Similarly, abf-2, lys-2, clec-4 and clec-65 were induced upon P. aeruginosa exposure independent of exogenous mitochondrial stress, which also required atfs-1 (Fig. 2eâ€“h). Furthermore, zip-2 transcript induction on P. aeruginosa17 was also partially impaired in atfs-1 mutant worms, suggesting atfs-1 can function upstream of zip-2 (Extended Data Fig. 4a).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1651}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We examined the role of ATFS-1 in the induction of innate immune genes during P. aeruginosa exposure rather than specifically during mitochondrial stress. Similarly, abf-2, lys-2, clec-4 and clec-65 were induced upon P. aeruginosa exposure independent of exogenous mitochondrial stress, which also required atfs-1 (Fig. 2eâ€“h). Furthermore, zip-2 transcript induction on P. aeruginosa17 was also partially impaired in atfs-1 mutant worms, suggesting atfs-1 can function upstream of zip-2 (Extended Data Fig. 4a).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1652}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We examined the role of ATFS-1 in the induction of innate immune genes during P. aeruginosa exposure rather than specifically during mitochondrial stress. Similarly, abf-2, lys-2, clec-4 and clec-65 were induced upon P. aeruginosa exposure independent of exogenous mitochondrial stress, which also required atfs-1 (Fig. 2eâ€“h). Furthermore, zip-2 transcript induction on P. aeruginosa17 was also partially impaired in atfs-1 mutant worms, suggesting atfs-1 can function upstream of zip-2 (Extended Data Fig. 4a).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1653}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We examined the role of ATFS-1 in the induction of innate immune genes during P. aeruginosa exposure rather than specifically during mitochondrial stress. Similarly, abf-2, lys-2, clec-4 and clec-65 were induced upon P. aeruginosa exposure independent of exogenous mitochondrial stress, which also required atfs-1 (Fig. 2eâ€“h). Furthermore, zip-2 transcript induction on P. aeruginosa17 was also partially impaired in atfs-1 mutant worms, suggesting atfs-1 can function upstream of zip-2 (Extended Data Fig. 4a).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1587}, end: {_id:1649}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We examined the role of ATFS-1 in the induction of innate immune genes during P. aeruginosa exposure rather than specifically during mitochondrial stress. Similarly, abf-2, lys-2, clec-4 and clec-65 were induced upon P. aeruginosa exposure independent of exogenous mitochondrial stress, which also required atfs-1 (Fig. 2eâ€“h). Furthermore, zip-2 transcript induction on P. aeruginosa17 was also partially impaired in atfs-1 mutant worms, suggesting atfs-1 can function upstream of zip-2 (Extended Data Fig. 4a).", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Transport related subgraph"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1655}, end: {_id:1658}, properties:{evidence:"While ROS are required for glucose metabolism and metastasis in triple-negative breast cancers (9), decreased SirT3 levels concomitant with high ROS levels are frequently observed in all breast cancers (8). Based on these findings, SirT3 is considered a tumor suppressor (5, 8). SirT3 regulates the activity of magnesium superoxide dismutase (MnSOD), which detoxifies superoxide to hydrogen peroxide (5, 7, 10,â€“14). Moreover, SirT3 has been linked to augmented transcription of MnSOD and catalase, both known targets of the transcription factor FOXO3A (15). The SirT3-dependent deacetylation of FOXO3A promotes both its nuclear translocation and its transcriptional activity (16, 17). Therefore, mechanistically, the reduction of SirT3 levels leads to an elevation in ROS levels by compromising the mitochondrial antioxidant machinery.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Apoptosis signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Breast Neoplasms"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1658}, end: {_id:394}, properties:{evidence:"Since FOXO3A is required for the transcription of both MnSOD and catalase, we also tested FOXO3A and found an increase in its levels (Fig. 3A and â€‹andB).B). We found that proteotoxic stress due to the accumulation of OTCÎ” also led to elevations of SirT3, FOXO3A, as well as LC3BII levels (Fig. 3I), suggesting that these responses are not specific to misfolded Endo G.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Mitochondrial subgraph", "Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "Response to oxidative stress"], annotationMeshdisease:["Breast Neoplasms"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1511}, end: {_id:1655}, properties:{evidence:"Since FOXO3A is required for the transcription of both MnSOD and catalase, we also tested FOXO3A and found an increase in its levels (Fig. 3A and â€‹andB).B). We found that proteotoxic stress due to the accumulation of OTCÎ” also led to elevations of SirT3, FOXO3A, as well as LC3BII levels (Fig. 3I), suggesting that these responses are not specific to misfolded Endo G.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Breast Neoplasms"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1511}, end: {_id:1658}, properties:{evidence:"Since FOXO3A is required for the transcription of both MnSOD and catalase, we also tested FOXO3A and found an increase in its levels (Fig. 3A and â€‹andB).B). We found that proteotoxic stress due to the accumulation of OTCÎ” also led to elevations of SirT3, FOXO3A, as well as LC3BII levels (Fig. 3I), suggesting that these responses are not specific to misfolded Endo G.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2014 Dec 18;516(7531):414-7. doi: 10.1038/nature13818. Epub 2014 Sep 28.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Breast Neoplasms"], pmid:"25274306", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25274306"}}, {start: {_id:1664}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Whereas K6â€�linked Ub conjugates on parkin appear to protect it from proteasomal degradation and to impede mitophagy, USP8â€�mediated removal of K6â€�linked Ub from parkin promotes parkin turnover and is required for mitophagy to proceed efficiently. Taken together, our work identifies USP8 and K6â€�linked Ub conjugates as key players in the regulation of parkinâ€�mediated mitophagy.", citationTitle:"EMBO J. 2014 Nov 3;33(21):2473-91. doi: 10.15252/embj.201489729. Epub 2014 Sep 12.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Mitophagy subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"25216678", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25216678"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:250}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 is involved in the phosphorylation of the mitochondrial proteins TRAP1 (TNF receptor-associated protein 1), Htr2a (5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) receptor 2a)/OMI (Plun-Favreau et al, 2007; Pridgeon et al, 2007) and also Parkin (Kim et al, 2008), and associates with Hsp90 (heat shock protein 90)/Cdc37 (cell division cycle 37) chaperones (Moriwaki et al, 2008; Weihofen et al, 2007).", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1712}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 is involved in the phosphorylation of the mitochondrial proteins TRAP1 (TNF receptor-associated protein 1), Htr2a (5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) receptor 2a)/OMI (Plun-Favreau et al, 2007; Pridgeon et al, 2007) and also Parkin (Kim et al, 2008), and associates with Hsp90 (heat shock protein 90)/Cdc37 (cell division cycle 37) chaperones (Moriwaki et al, 2008; Weihofen et al, 2007).", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph", "G-protein-mediated signaling"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1713}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 is involved in the phosphorylation of the mitochondrial proteins TRAP1 (TNF receptor-associated protein 1), Htr2a (5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) receptor 2a)/OMI (Plun-Favreau et al, 2007; Pridgeon et al, 2007) and also Parkin (Kim et al, 2008), and associates with Hsp90 (heat shock protein 90)/Cdc37 (cell division cycle 37) chaperones (Moriwaki et al, 2008; Weihofen et al, 2007).", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph", "G-protein-mediated signaling"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1459}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"PINK1 is involved in the phosphorylation of the mitochondrial proteins TRAP1 (TNF receptor-associated protein 1), Htr2a (5-hydroxytryptamine (serotonin) receptor 2a)/OMI (Plun-Favreau et al, 2007; Pridgeon et al, 2007) and also Parkin (Kim et al, 2008), and associates with Hsp90 (heat shock protein 90)/Cdc37 (cell division cycle 37) chaperones (Moriwaki et al, 2008; Weihofen et al, 2007).", citationTitle:"EMBO Mol Med. 2009 May;1(2):99-111. doi: 10.1002/emmm.200900006.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"20049710", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20049710"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1520}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We found that parkin null mutants showed a significant overall slowing of mitochondrial protein turnover, similar to but less severe than the slowing seen in autophagy- deficient Atg7 mutants, consistent with the model that Parkin acts upstream of Atg7 to promote mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These studies showed that loss of mitochondrial membrane potential (depolarization) leads to accumulation of PINK1 on the mitochondrial outer membrane, which triggers recruitment of Parkin to the mitochondria.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondrial Membranes"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We found that parkin mutant flies had a significantly decreased rate of mitochondrial protein turnover, similar to the decrease produced by general autophagy blockade, supporting the hypothesis that Parkin promotes mitochondrial turnover through autophagy.", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Consistent with our hypothesis, mitochondrial proteins in parkin and Atg7 mutants had significantly prolonged half-lives compared with controls", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:1520}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Consistent with our hypothesis, mitochondrial proteins in parkin and Atg7 mutants had significantly prolonged half-lives compared with controls", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Autophagy signaling subgraph"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:1520}, end: {_id:1759}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Also as hypothesized, lack of Atg7 had a stronger effect on mitochondrial protein turnover than lack of Parkin", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Previous work has shown that PINK1 is required for Parkin-mediated mitophagy after treatment of cultured cells with mitochondrial depolarizing agents (11, 12), and genetic studies in Drosophila show that PINK1 null mutants have similar phenotypes to parkin null mutants", citationTitle:"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2013 Apr 16;110(16):6400-5. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1221132110. Epub 2013 Mar 18.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["7227"], pmid:"23509287", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23509287"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"The disruption of mitochondrial membrane (MM) potential (Î”Î¨m) by uncouplers or oxidative stress leads to PINK1 accumulation on the outer MM (OMM), the PINK1-dependent recruitment and activation of Parkin, the ubiquitylation and proteasome-dependent degradation of nearby substrates, and the initiation of mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2015 Oct;22(10):1563-76. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2014.224. Epub 2015 Jan 16.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25591737", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25591737"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1404}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Ubiquitin-dependent regulation of the TOM-dependent import of HSD17B10 by Parkin, possibly in cooperation with PINK1, may ensure the correct maintenance of HSD17B10 homeostasis in conditions of low mitochondrial import efficiency.", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2015 Oct;22(10):1563-76. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2014.224. Epub 2015 Jan 16.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Mitochondrial subgraph"], pmid:"25591737", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25591737"}}, {start: {_id:221}, end: {_id:1404}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Ubiquitin-dependent regulation of the TOM-dependent import of HSD17B10 by Parkin, possibly in cooperation with PINK1, may ensure the correct maintenance of HSD17B10 homeostasis in conditions of low mitochondrial import efficiency.", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2015 Oct;22(10):1563-76. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2014.224. Epub 2015 Jan 16.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"25591737", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25591737"}}, {start: {_id:271}, end: {_id:1404}, properties:{evidence:"Parkin maintains mitochondrial levels of the protective Parkinson's disease-related enzyme 17-Î² hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 10", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2015 Oct;22(10):1563-76. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2014.224. Epub 2015 Jan 16.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Mitochondrial subgraph"], pmid:"25591737", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25591737"}}, {start: {_id:1839}, end: {_id:221}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Translationally repressed nRCC mRNAs are localized in a PINK1/Tom20- dependent manner to mitochondrial outer membrane, where they are derepressed and activated by PINK1/Parkin through displacement of translation repressors, including Pumilio and Glorund/ hnRNP-F, a Parkin substrate, and enhanced binding of activators such as eIF4G.", citationTitle:"Cell Metab. 2015 Jan 6;21(1):95-108. doi: 10.1016/j.cmet.2014.12.007.", annotationSubgraph:["PINK1 subgraph", "Pre-translational events"], pmid:"25565208", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25565208"}}] AS row
