UNWIND [{start: {_id:74}, end: {_id:75}, properties:{evidence:"However, the N-terminally truncated Grb10 (BPS-SH2) shows no effect on receptor phosphorylation or glucose uptake, implying that the N-terminal domains of Grb10 is essential to its regulatory effect on IR signaling [12]. Recent studies via yeast two-hybrid screening have demonstrated that the N-terminus of Grb10 interacts with GYF proteins (GIGYF1 and GIGYF2), which are two novel and homologous proline motif binding proteins. The GIGYF1 protein can be recruited to the activated insulin and IGF1 receptors through binding to the N-terminus of Grb10. Subsequent studies using GIGYF2 null mice models showed that GIGYF2 gene disruption in mice led to inhibition of IGF1-stimulated IGF1R tyrosine phosphorylation and consequently, the manifestation of neurodegeneration [14]. Those findings suggest that Grb10 and GIGYF2 may act cooperatively to regulate IGF1R signaling. In addition, a high level of GIGYF2 expression is presented in the pancreas and testis as well as brain (including the hippocampus) of adult mouse, thus supporting a vital role of GIGYF2 in the regulation of IGF1R signaling in the central NervousSystem (CNS) [15]. Most recently, several lines of evidence have shown that GIGYF2 gene mutations are closely linked to human familial Parkinson's disease, one of the most common neurodegenerative disorders, thus further supporting the notion that GIGYF2 may play a critical role in neuronal degradation in CNS.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2014 Sep 30;9(9):e108559. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0108559. eCollection 2014.", annotationSubgraph:["GRB10 subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"25268761", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25268761"}}, {start: {_id:83}, end: {_id:85}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In addition to inhibiting insulin receptor and IGF1R kinase activity by directly binding to the receptors, GRB10 can also negatively regulate insulin and IGF1 signaling by mediating insulin receptor and IGF1R degradation through ubiquitination.", citationTitle:"J Biol Chem. 2010 Dec 31;285(53):42130-9. doi: 10.1074/jbc.M110.143412. Epub 2010 Oct 26.", annotationSubgraph:["GRB10 subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"20980250", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20980250"}}, {start: {_id:68}, end: {_id:86}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"In addition to inhibiting insulin receptor and IGF1R kinase activity by directly binding to the receptors, GRB10 can also negatively regulate insulin and IGF1 signaling by mediating insulin receptor and IGF1R degradation through ubiquitination.", citationTitle:"J Biol Chem. 2010 Dec 31;285(53):42130-9. doi: 10.1074/jbc.M110.143412. Epub 2010 Oct 26.", annotationSubgraph:["GRB10 subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"20980250", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20980250"}}, {start: {_id:91}, end: {_id:86}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"It has been shown that GRB10 can interact with the C2 domain of the E3 ubiquitin ligase NEDD4 through its Src homology 2 (SH2) domain. Therefore, GRB10 might act as a connector, bringing NEDD4 close to IGF1R to facilitate the ubiquitination of IGF1R by NEDD4.", citationTitle:"J Biol Chem. 2010 Dec 31;285(53):42130-9. doi: 10.1074/jbc.M110.143412. Epub 2010 Oct 26.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "GRB10 subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"20980250", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20980250"}}, {start: {_id:107}, end: {_id:110}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that the interaction of DLL-1 on mouse joint macrophages with Notch-2 on mouse FLS enhances the production of IL-6 and MMP-3. Therefore, suppression of IL-6, GM-CSF, and MMP-3 production by DLL-1 blockade might be responsible for the amelioration of arthritis in a mouse model of RA.", citationTitle:"Arthritis Rheumatol. 2014 Oct;66(10):2751-61. doi: 10.1002/art.38743.", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24943093", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24943093"}}, {start: {_id:107}, end: {_id:111}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that the interaction of DLL-1 on mouse joint macrophages with Notch-2 on mouse FLS enhances the production of IL-6 and MMP-3. Therefore, suppression of IL-6, GM-CSF, and MMP-3 production by DLL-1 blockade might be responsible for the amelioration of arthritis in a mouse model of RA.", citationTitle:"Arthritis Rheumatol. 2014 Oct;66(10):2751-61. doi: 10.1002/art.38743.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24943093", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24943093"}}, {start: {_id:714}, end: {_id:137}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Together, these changes could impair copper binding and contribute to alpha-synuclein aggregation (Fig. 4A).", citationTitle:"Biochemical and biophysical research communications; Vol. 441; Iss. 2", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], pmid:"24140056", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24140056"}}, {start: {_id:322}, end: {_id:321}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Furthermore, it has been shown that LRRK2 regulates mitochondrial dynamics by increasing mitochondrial DLP1 (dynamin-like kinase 1) through a direct interaction with DLP1 [21].", citationTitle:"Oxid Med Cell Longev. 2013;2013:817807. doi: 10.1155/2013/817807. Epub 2013 Mar 14.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "LRRK2 subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], annotationCell:["fibroblast"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Mitochondria"], pmid:"23577227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23577227"}}, {start: {_id:74}, end: {_id:75}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"However, the N-terminally truncated Grb10 (BPS-SH2) shows no effect on receptor phosphorylation or glucose uptake, implying that the N-terminal domains of Grb10 is essential to its regulatory effect on IR signaling [12]. Recent studies via yeast two-hybrid screening have demonstrated that the N-terminus of Grb10 interacts with GYF proteins (GIGYF1 and GIGYF2), which are two novel and homologous proline motif binding proteins. The GIGYF1 protein can be recruited to the activated insulin and IGF1 receptors through binding to the N-terminus of Grb10. Subsequent studies using GIGYF2 null mice models showed that GIGYF2 gene disruption in mice led to inhibition of IGF1-stimulated IGF1R tyrosine phosphorylation and consequently, the manifestation of neurodegeneration [14]. Those findings suggest that Grb10 and GIGYF2 may act cooperatively to regulate IGF1R signaling. In addition, a high level of GIGYF2 expression is presented in the pancreas and testis as well as brain (including the hippocampus) of adult mouse, thus supporting a vital role of GIGYF2 in the regulation of IGF1R signaling in the central nervous system (CNS) [15]. Most recently, several lines of evidence have shown that GIGYF2 gene mutations are closely linked to human familial Parkinson's disease, one of the most common neurodegenerative disorders, thus further supporting the notion that GIGYF2 may play a critical role in neuronal degradation in CNS.", citationTitle:"PLoS One. 2014 Sep 30;9(9):e108559. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0108559. eCollection 2014.", annotationSubgraph:["GRB10 subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], pmid:"25268761", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25268761"}}, {start: {_id:944}, end: {_id:110}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that the interaction of DLL-1 on mouse joint macrophages with Notch-2 on mouse FLS enhances the production of IL-6 and MMP-3. Therefore, suppression of IL-6, GM-CSF, and MMP-3 production by DLL-1 blockade might be responsible for the amelioration of arthritis in a mouse model of RA.", citationTitle:"Arthritis Rheumatol. 2014 Oct;66(10):2751-61. doi: 10.1002/art.38743.", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24943093", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24943093"}}, {start: {_id:944}, end: {_id:111}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"These results suggest that the interaction of DLL-1 on mouse joint macrophages with Notch-2 on mouse FLS enhances the production of IL-6 and MMP-3. Therefore, suppression of IL-6, GM-CSF, and MMP-3 production by DLL-1 blockade might be responsible for the amelioration of arthritis in a mouse model of RA.", citationTitle:"Arthritis Rheumatol. 2014 Oct;66(10):2751-61. doi: 10.1002/art.38743.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph", "Notch signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24943093", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24943093"}}, {start: {_id:1080}, end: {_id:1081}, properties:{evidence:"Binding of the parkin ubiquitin-like (Ubl) domain to the Eps15 ubiquitin-interacting motifs (UIMs) is required for parkin-mediated Eps15 ubiquitination.", citationComment:"A regulated interaction with the UIM protein Eps15 implicates parkin in EGF receptor trafficking and PI(3)K-Akt signalling.", citationTitle:"Nature cell biology; Vol. 8; Iss. 8", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of cytoskeleton subgraph", "Ubiquitin subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Parkinson Disease"], citationPubDate:"2006-08-01", pmid:"16862145", citationAuthorList:"Lara Fallon|Catherine M L BâˆšÂ©langer|Amadou T Corera|Maria Kontogiannea|Elsa Regan-Klapisz|France Moreau|Jarno Voortman|Michael Haber|GeneviâˆšÂ®ve Rouleau|Thorhildur Thorarinsdottir|Alexis Brice|Paul M P van Bergen En Henegouwen|Edward A Fon", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16862145"}}, {start: {_id:1129}, end: {_id:1130}, properties:{evidence:"We found that parkin acts as an E3 ubiquitin ligase to ubiquitinate FAF1 both in vitro and at cellular level, identifying FAF1 as a direct substrate of parkin.", citationComment:"Accumulation of the parkin substrate, FAF1, plays a key role in the dopaminergic neurodegeneration.", citationTitle:"Human molecular genetics; Vol. 22; Iss. 8", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Apoptosis signaling subgraph"], citationPubDate:"2013-04-15", pmid:"23307929", citationAuthorList:"Jee-Won Sul|Min-Young Park|Juhee Shin|Young-Ran Kim|Sung-Eun Yoo|Young-Yun Kong|Ki-Sun Kwon|Young Ho Lee|Eunhee Kim", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23307929"}}, {start: {_id:1614}, end: {_id:1616}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Mutations in PTEN-induced putative kinase 1 (PINK1) or parkin cause autosomal recessive forms of Parkinson's disease.Recent work suggests that loss of mitochondrial membrane potential stabilizes PINK1 and that accumulated PINK1 recruits parkin from the cytoplasm to mitochondria for elimination of depolarized mitochondria, which is known as mitophagy.In this study, we find that PINK1 forms a complex with sterile ÃŽÂ± and TIR motif containing 1 (SARM1) and tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor 6 (TRAF6), which is important for import of PINK1 in the outer membrane and stabilization of PINK1 on depolarized mitochondria. SARM1, which is known to be an adaptor protein for Toll-like receptor, binds to PINK1 and promotes TRAF6-mediated lysine 63 chain ubiquitination of PINK1 at lysine 433. Down-regulation of SARM1 and TRAF6 abrogates accumulation of PINK1, followed by recruitment of parkin to damaged mitochondria. Some pathogenic mutations of PINK1 reduce the complex formation and ubiquitination.These results indicate that association of PINK1 with SARM1 and TRAF6 is an important step for mitophagy.", citationTitle:"Mol Biol Cell. 2013 Sep;24(18):2772-84. doi: 10.1091/mbc.E13-01-0016. Epub 2013 Jul 24.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph", "PINK1 subgraph"], pmid:"23885119", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23885119"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"AIF is indispensable for translation-coupled mitochondrial import of CHCHD4", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"AIF depletion by several non-overlapping siRNAs failed to affect the mitochondrial transmembrane potential (Joza et al., 2001; Figure S4C), yet caused a reduction of CHCHD4 protein expression with a delay of several days (Figures 5A, 5B, and S4D). Re-introduction of AIF expression by means of non- interferable plasmid constructs suppressed the effects of an AIF-specific siRNA on CHCHD4 expression (Figures S4A and S4B).", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Mitochondrial subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:1881}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"As shown, AIF is required for the expression of CHCHD4 protein.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:1589}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"We transfected this new construct into cells and observed that the co- translation of both recombinant CHCHD4 (upstream of 2A) and GFP (downstream of 2A) was attenuated when AIF was depleted.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1877}, end: {_id:1589}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"Hence, by interfering with the co-translational import of CHCHD4 into mitochondria, AIF depletion also reduced the translation of GFP in this system", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}, {start: {_id:1909}, end: {_id:1889}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"It is possible, but still remains to be determined, that CHCHD4 bound to AIF maintains its catalytic activity by transiently interacting with its partner enzyme (Erv1) and its substrates.", citationTitle:"Mol Cell. 2015 Jun 18;58(6):1001-14. doi: 10.1016/j.molcel.2015.04.020. Epub 2015 May 21.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Chaperone subgraph"], pmid:"26004228", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26004228"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1918}, end: {_id:271}, properties:{annotationCondition:["Normal Healthy State"], evidence:"ATF4 binds to the parkin promoter and mediates parkin upregulation in response to ER and mitochondrial stress.", citationTitle:"Cell Death Differ. 2011 May;18(5):769-82. doi: 10.1038/cdd.2010.142. Epub 2010 Nov 26.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin subgraph", "Unfolded protein response subgraph"], pmid:"21113145", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21113145"}}, {start: {_id:1996}, end: {_id:1998}, properties:{evidence:"Interaction with TRAF6 leads to ECSIT ubiquitination and enrichment at the mitochondrial periphery, resulting in increased mitochondrial and cellular ROS generation. ECSIT- and TRAF6-depleted macrophages have decreased levels of TLR-induced ROS and are significantly impaired in their ability to kill intracellular bacteria.", citationTitle:"Nature. 2011 Apr 28;472(7344):476-80. doi:10.1038/nature09973.", annotationSubgraph:["Unfolded protein response subgraph", "Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "MAPK subgraph"], annotationCell:["macrophage"], pmid:"21525932", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21525932"}}, {start: {_id:2095}, end: {_id:2101}, properties:{evidence:"Overexpression of each factors, Nurr1, Pitx3, and Lmx1a robustly promoted the dopaminergic differentiation of ESC-NP cells exposed to sonic hedgehog (SHH) and fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8). In addition, each of these factors directly interacts with potential binding sites within the tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) gene and activated its promoter activity. Interestingly, however, overexpression of Nurr1, but not of Pitx3 or Lmx1a, generated a significant number of nonneuronal TH-positive cells. In line with this, Pitx3 and Lmx1a, but not Nurr1, induced expression of the Ngn2 gene, which is critical for neurogenesis", citationTitle:"Stem Cells Dev. 2014 Mar 1;23(5):477-87. doi: 10.1089/scd.2013.0406. Epub 2013 Dec 4.", annotationSubgraph:["Dopaminergic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Midbrain Raphe Nuclei"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24172139", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24172139"}}] AS row
